INSERT INTO chapter_narration (slug, anchor_key, audio_key, duration_s, source_hash, voice, cues_json) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'a prayer for the road', 'ayyuhal-walad/narration/a-prayer-for-the-road.mp3', '277.56', 'cfb729200059ae624c147eb96cbc9b9cef16e035a0f9b1238f4f18c7a7898e00', 'aria', '[{"idx": 0, "blockIndex": 0, "startS": 0.0, "endS": 13.032}, {"idx": 1, "blockIndex": 1, "startS": 13.032, "endS": 47.424}, {"idx": 2, "blockIndex": 3, "startS": 47.424, "endS": 87.48}, {"idx": 3, "blockIndex": 4, "startS": 87.48, "endS": 119.04}, {"idx": 4, "blockIndex": 5, "startS": 119.04, "endS": 218.136}, {"idx": 5, "blockIndex": 6, "startS": 218.136, "endS": 226.344}, {"idx": 6, "blockIndex": 7, "startS": 226.344, "endS": 277.56}]');
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 1, 'Knowledge Without Action', 'The opening of Imam al-Ghazali''s letter *Ayyuha al-Walad* — the diagnosis half of his reply to a long-time disciple who wrote asking which of the many sciences he had been taught would actually save him on the Day of Judgment. Ghazali refuses the assumption that more teaching is the deficit (*if you have not received benefit from the counsels of the Prophet, then tell me — what knowledge have you truly acquired from me?*) and presses a single verdict from seven angles: the Prophet''s three warnings on useless knowledge and the forty-year clock and the scholar''s severest torment; the dream of Shaykh Junaid Baghdadi whose lifetime of discourse weighed nothing while a small number of voluntary Tahajjud prayers were of benefit; the lion-in-the- jungle parable and the unswallowed medicine; the Quranic verses on the atom''s weight and the verses of faith joined to action; the hadith that Islam is *built upon* five pillars and that faith is testimony-tongue-heart-and-action-of-limbs; the careful clarification that this is not works-righteousness but the condition of receiving Mercy (*the Mercy of Allah is close to the doers of good*); and the story of the worshipper among the Children of Israel whose unconditional service drew the forgiveness the angel''s test had pretended to withhold. The episode closes on the Prophetic hinge — *take account of yourselves before you are taken to account* — and the three closing witnesses on acting without self-admiration. The lion is coming; the swords are in the hand; whether the arm will swing is the work of the next episode.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 2, 'The Path of Obedience and Struggle', 'The second half of the opening movement of *Ayyuha al-Walad* — the pivot from diagnosis to cure. Ghazali turns the disciple from the verdict that knowledge alone will not save him to the inhabited practice of the dawn watch: Hasan al-Basri''s involuntary sigh at the cold cup; the standing pre-dawn proclamation *is there anyone who repents, anyone who asks of Me, anyone who seeks My forgiveness?*; the Quranic command addressed directly to the Prophet ﷺ to perform *Tahajjud*; the four angels of the four stations of the night; the wind Allah has created that carries the voices of His servants at the hour before dawn; and Luqman''s testament to his own son not to let the rooster prove more intelligent than he. From the dawn watch Ghazali turns to the principle that worship is another name for obedience to the Prophetic *Shari''ah*, and works it out through four edge-cases — the fast on Eid as sin, the prayer at a forbidden hour as transgression, the prayer on unlawfully occupied ground, and the lawful intimacy between husband and wife that the uninformed would judge as appetite — to establish that the Prophetic frame is the test of whether any act is worship at all. He then turns to the inner condition: the Path cannot be travelled by accumulated knowledge, only by *mujahadah*, and the lower self must be severed with the sword of struggle. The polemic against the simulation of Sufism — and the parenthetical defense of its true practice — closes that movement. The episode ends on Ghazali''s first answer to the disciple''s second question: the four conditions of the sincere seeker, sealed by Shibli''s hadith of the four proportions which contains *the knowledge of all who came first and all who came last*. The disciple now knows what watch to keep, what rule to follow, what struggle to wage, and what four conditions frame the life.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 3, 'The Shaykh and the Disciple''s Rule of Life', 'The practical rule of life Imam al-Ghazali hands his disciple. Three braided strands. First, the eight benefits Hatim ibn Ism brought back to his master Shaykh Shafeeq al-Balkhi from long inward observation of the people of the world — each one anchored in a Quranic verse, each one the same shape: *I looked, I weighed against the Book, I changed what I did.* The only beloved who descends into the grave is one''s own righteous deeds; the Nafs must be disciplined under Quran 79:40–41; wealth must be sent ahead under Quran 16:96; Taqwa is the only true nobility under Quran 49:13; contentment with the assigned portion under Quran 43:32; Shaytan alone is the enemy under Quran 35:6 and the covenant verse; sustenance is Allah''s responsibility under Quran 11:6; Tawakkul on the One who suffices under Quran 65:3. Shaykh Shafeeq''s verdict on the eight is that whoever acts on them has acted on all four sacred Books — Tawrat, Injil, Zabur, Quran. Second, the figure at the center of the seeker''s life — the Shaykh al-Kamil, the perfected guide whose Bay''a chains unbroken back to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; the full catalog of his qualifications; the bright line that he cannot command anything forbidden by the Shari''ah; the inner etiquette of no doubt in the heart — with the honest-withdrawal clause when the inward and outward cannot agree. Third, the inner vocabulary of the Path — Ghazali''s brief, precise definitions of Tasawwuf (Shari''ah + good toward creation), servitude (obey, accept, dissolve the *I want*), Tawakkul (certitude in what is promised), and Ikhlas (deeds for Allah alone, with the metaphysical cure for Riya). The chapter closes with Ghazali''s instruction that the rest of the answers come through practice rather than further questioning — three Quranic verses on not hastening, the verb *travel* from Quran on those who refuse to set out, and Shaykh Zun Noon Misri''s line that the path is the soul actually offered, not the beautiful sayings of the Sufis. The eight admonitions belong to the next episode.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 4, 'Eight Admonitions and a Closing Prayer', 'The master''s closing brief to his beloved disciple. Eight admonitions, structured as four things to refrain from and four things to actively attend to — so that knowledge does not turn against the scholar on the Day of Judgment. The four refrainings: do not engage in religious debate (with the two narrow signs of right intention if the rare licit argument arises, and the long aside on the four categories of patients — only the obedient, sharp, intelligent seeker is worth answering); do not preach (with the *Ya Ibna Maryama* address to Jesus and the *Al-Hazr! Al-Hazr!* flood image governing what a sermon must be — fear, the Hereafter, Munkar and Nakir, the Mizan, the Sirat — and the *summoning of God''s creation from this toward that* litany governing what the preacher''s heart must seek); do not greet or even look upon kings, the wealthy, or rulers (with the hadith that praying for an oppressor''s long life is, in effect, praying for the prolongation of oppression); and do not accept their gifts even from lawful sources (with the *Shaytan*-counsel about routing money through the poor as the lure itself). The four takings-up: an intimate relationship with Allah audited by the household-servant analogy; right relating to the slaves of Allah audited by the sacred hadith *my slave''s faith is never complete until he loves for all human beings what he loves for himself*; focused study under the one-week-to-live thought experiment and the king-is-visiting house-cleaning image anchored by *Allah does not look at your faces or your outward actions, but at your hearts and your intentions*; and not accumulating more than one year''s provision, with the Prophet ﷺ''s calibrated practice for the Azwaj al-Mutahharat and ''Aisha Siddiqa. The chapter closes with the long supplication the disciple is to recite after every Salaat — the abundance block, the correction block, and the eleven divine names sealing the letter — and with the master''s final naming of Al-Malik and Al-Wahhab as the help under which the book has been written. The letter ends here.
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'knowledge that will not save you', 'student:d9e814516a86fc1b', 1, 'I know you may have read that this verse was abrogated. Let us grant…', 'I know you may have read that this verse was abrogated. Let us grant that it was.', '<p>The verse <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَأَن لَّيْسَ لِلْإِنسَنِ إِلَّا مَا سَعَىٰ</span> appears within a passage describing the ancient scrolls (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صحف</span>) of Ibrahim and Musa. Because of this, some early scholars held that <strong>it stated a law for an earlier community</strong>, not a universal ruling for this one — since Islam permits intercession and the raising of a believer''s children through their parent''s faith. The scholar most often cited on this is Ibn Abbas, who pointed to a verse of Al-Tur as the abrogating text:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَاتَّبَعَتْهُمْ ذُرِّيَّتُهُم بِإِيمَانٍ أَلْحَقْنَا بِهِمْ ذُرِّيَّتَهُمْ</span> &quot;And those who believed and whose descendants followed them in faith — We will join their descendants to them.&quot; Al-Tur 52:21</p><p>Under this verse, a child is raised to the station of a believing parent without having performed equal deeds of their own. Al-Shafi''i and the many scholars who followed him took the opposite view: the verse was never abrogated and applies fully to every Muslim.</p><blockquote><p>And do not do mischief on the earth, after it has been set in order, and invoke Him with fear and hope; Surely, Allah''s Mercy is (ever) near unto the good&amp;shy;doers.</p><p>Al-A''raf 7:56), and placing oneself within reach of that mercy requires genuine striving (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مجاهدة</span>).</p></blockquote><p><strong>A person who strives is not earning paradise as a wage</strong> — they are making themselves ready to receive a gift that only Allah gives. Faith without action cannot receive what action opens the door to.</p>', '["سعى (to strive, to hasten): from the root س-ع-ي, meaning to walk briskly toward something with purpose. The word carries directed motion — effort aimed at a destination. From it comes the ritual سعي between Safa and Marwa, which enacts in the body what the concept demands of the soul: you do not wait to be carried.", "نسخ (abrogation): from the root ن-س-خ, meaning to transcribe or copy, then to replace one text with another. As a legal term it means a later divine ruling supersedes an earlier one. The root''s imagery — one inscription overwriting another — captures how scholars understood revelation as a living word that could develop across time.", "مجاهدة (sustained striving): from the root ج-ه-د, meaning to exert oneself to the very limit. The Form III verbal noun pattern suggests a struggle held against resistance, as though the self and its lower pulls are two parties locked in effort. Its siblings are جهاد (striving in Allah''s cause) and اجتهاد (independent legal reasoning through maximum effort)."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'knowledge that will not save you', 'student:4dff3e915392a50e', 2, 'unless that servant makes himself deserving of Allah''s mercy through…', 'unless that servant makes himself deserving of Allah''s mercy through worship and obedience, he will not stand in a position to receive that mercy and favor', '<p>Imam Ghazali draws a distinction that is easy to walk past. He is not saying that a servant''s good deeds purchase divine mercy — that would reduce Allah to a merchant and worship to a currency. What he is pointing to is <strong>receptivity</strong>: worship does not move Allah''s mercy toward the servant; it moves the servant into a state where Allah''s mercy, already present, can actually be received.</p><p>Sunlight never stops shining. It does not give more warmth to one patch of earth because that earth deserves it. Yet a clean mirror and a muddy stone receive the very same light entirely differently. Worship and obedience are the <strong>polishing</strong>: they do not change what Allah gives; they change what the servant can take in.</p><p>The verse Imam Ghazali cites makes this visible:</p><p>&quot;Indeed, the mercy of Allah is near to those who do good.&quot; Al-A''raf 7:56</p><p>It does not say mercy is given as a reward to those who earned it. It says mercy is <strong>near</strong> — as though it was never distant, and the one who does good has simply turned to face what was already waiting there for them.</p><p>Capacity, worthiness, and grace collapse into one movement here. Worship <strong>orients</strong> the heart; that orientation is what worthiness means; grace fills whatever the orientation has opened. A servant who never worships has not been withheld from by Allah — he has simply left the vessel upturned.</p>', '["رحمة (mercy): from root ر-ح-م, whose core sense is رَحِم, the womb — a space that shelters and sustains life from within. Allah''s mercy carries this same depth in the language itself: not a reward handed across a counter, but an enveloping warmth the creature already lives inside.", "عبادة (worship): from root ع-ب-د, meaning to serve absolutely, as a slave serves a master. The worshipper (عَابِد) is literally one who has placed themselves in the posture of belonging entirely to another — an act of inner surrender before it is an act of outward ritual.", "إحسان (excellence in doing good): from root ح-س-ن, meaning beautiful. The محسنين the verse names are not merely good-doers but those whose actions carry the quality of beauty — the same root gives حُسْن, beauty. This is the register in which human effort begins to mirror something of the divine."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'the striving that mercy meets', 'student:97828ebd7c329c1e', 1, 'whoever supposes that he will arrive by his own effort alone presumes…', 'whoever supposes that he will arrive by his own effort alone presumes himself in no need', '<p>Effort is not optional — to seek Paradise without deeds, Hasan al-Basri said, is itself a sin. But the one who believes his striving alone will carry him has made himself <strong>into someone who needs nothing from Allah</strong>, and that posture shuts the door to mercy as surely as laziness does.</p><p>What draws mercy is the quality the man in the story shows after the angel delivers his crushing verdict. He does not bargain, does not despair, and does not raise his seventy years of worship as a claim. He says simply: we were made to serve Him, and it is only right that we serve Him, <strong>whatever comes of it</strong>. That unclenching — offering effort as something owed rather than something that purchases a reward — is the act beyond striving.</p><p>The passage names this quality directly:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَلَامَةُ الْحَقِيقَةِ تَرْكُ مُلَاحَظَةِ الْعَمَلِ لَا تَرْكُ الْعَمَلِ</span> &quot;The mark of realized truth is to leave off admiring one''s own action, not to leave off the action itself.&quot;</p><p>The danger is not in working hard. It is in watching yourself work hard — measuring your devotion, cataloguing your sacrifices, and then presenting that catalogue before Allah as a claim. <strong>Self-regard fills the room that mercy needs to enter.</strong></p><p>So the two work together: effort reaches the door, and surrender is the hand that knocks. The worshipper knocked — he said &quot;whatever comes of it&quot; and meant it — and the response came: We too <strong>will not turn Our Face away from him</strong>.</p>', '["ظَنَّ (root ظ-ن-ن): to hold an opinion without certainty. Lane uses ظَنّ for conjecture — a view held without full knowledge. Maulana Ali applies it to both the dreamer and the self-reliant; both are still guessing. The root encodes humility: when you ظَنَّ, you admit that the full picture belongs to someone else.", "يَصِلُ (root و-ص-ل): to arrive, but rooted in connection. Lane''s Lexicon defines صِلَةُ الرَّحِمِ as maintaining bonds of kinship through kindness and gentleness. So يَصِلُ is not merely reaching a destination — it carries the resonance of reconnecting. The arrival at Allah is a restored bond, reframing the journey as a return to relationship, not the completion of a transaction.", "مَنّ (root م-ن-ن): to bestow a favor freely, without the recipient having earned it. مَنَّ appears in the Quran as Allah''s unrestricted giving. The same root generates مَمْنُون — one who carries gratitude for an unearned gift. The journey ends not with a settled account but with the traveller becoming مَمْنُون, holding something he could never have purchased."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'the striving that mercy meets', 'student:1b613bb1754ca4bf', 2, 'do what you wish, for you shall surely be recompensed for it', 'do what you wish, for you shall surely be recompensed for it', '<p>The hadith that closes this chapter carries <strong>a double edge the chapter itself has been sharpening all along</strong>. The surface reading appears to grant permission — live as you wish, love as you wish, do as you wish — but the word &quot;recompensed&quot; (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مَجْزِيٌّ</span>) turns that apparent freedom into a mirror held up to conscience. Every choice casts a shadow forward. The warning is real: whoever follows the desires of the soul and indulges vain hopes upon Allah, as the Prophet described the fool only lines earlier, will find those very deeds waiting for him.</p><p>But <strong>the chapter''s entire arc has been building toward something more urgent than a warning</strong>. It opened with the worshipper who served Allah for seventy years and, when told his worship had earned him nothing, simply said: we were created to serve, and we shall serve regardless. It moved through the teaching of Maulana Ali — neither the dreamer who expects arrival without striving, nor the self-satisfied one who thinks striving alone is enough. It arrived at the intimate of Allah who said: the mark of realized truth is to stop admiring your own action, not to stop acting. Every station in that journey has been an encouragement forward, not a fence around the past.</p><p>So when the chapter closes with &quot;do what you wish, for you shall be recompensed,&quot; it speaks most directly to the one who is already striving — the student who has spent nights over books, whose motive the author earlier put to the test. The closing word is less a threat and more a <strong>promise with weight</strong>: your deeds are real, your effort is seen, and none of it dissolves into the air. The warning is inseparable from this, because the same recompense that rewards sincere striving repays careless desire.</p><p>What the chapter intends as its final word is not dread but <strong>accountability made beautiful</strong> — the recognition that the universe is not indifferent to what we do. The intelligent person, as the Prophet said, calls his own soul to account and works for what lies after death. The hadith seals that teaching: the freedom in &quot;do what you wish&quot; is no longer reckless — it is the freedom of someone who already knows what they wish for.</p>', NULL);
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'the hours before dawn', 'student:7cddf49f52b78d5e', 1, 'The Throne of the Most Merciful trembled at the death of Sa''d ibn Muadh', 'The Throne of the Most Merciful trembled at the death of Sa''d ibn Muadh', '<p>The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرْش</span> in Islamic understanding is not a piece of furniture sitting at the edge of the sky. It is the highest expression of divine sovereignty over all of creation — the summit from which every act of mercy and authority descends into the world below. When we hear that the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرْش</span> trembled at the death of Sa''d ibn Muadh, we are hearing not of a tremor alone but of a <strong>cosmic event that registered at the very apex of existence</strong>.</p><p>The connection to the upward flight of the soul runs through the image Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رض</span>) has given us: our bodies are cages for birds, or stables for beasts. A bird does not leave its cage by crawling; it takes flight the instant the door opens. Sa''d had spent his life in complete service — his loyalty, his courage, his blood, given from wounds received in battle. <strong>His soul had already oriented itself entirely toward the higher world</strong> while still inside the body, so when death came and the cage opened, what took flight was a presence the highest point of creation trembled to receive.</p><p>The soul''s ascent and the Throne''s response are one event, seen from two vantage points. From below, we witness a man dying. From the level of the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرْش</span>, creation is receiving a soul that has made itself worthy of that height. The trembling is not grief; it is not surprise. It is the response of the highest to the arrival of one who held nothing back. <strong>Sa''d did not merely die — he arrived.</strong></p><p>We are told that a soul among the birds of the higher world takes flight when the divine call of return is sounded. The trembling of the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَرْش</span> is what that return sounds like from above. <strong>The measure of a death is not how peacefully the body is laid down</strong> — it is how completely the soul departing it has already made itself a thing of the world it is flying toward.</p>', '["عَرْش (Throne): from the root ع-ر-ش, whose core sense is a raised canopy or shade-structure — reeds or palm-sticks built overhead for shelter. The Quranic يَعْرِشُ means to construct such a covering. Applied to Allah''s Throne, the root carries the image of a supreme canopy arching over creation, from which authority and mercy descend.", "سَعْد (Sa''d): from the root س-ع-د, yielding سَعَادَة, felicity, and سَعِيد, the felicitous one — a life oriented toward divine favour. That the Throne trembles for a man whose very name means felicity is not incidental; the name was the announcement, and his death was its fulfilment.", "مُعَاذ (Muadh): from the root ع-و-ذ — to seek refuge or shelter in something greater than oneself. مَعَاذ is a place of refuge; عُذْ is the command ''seek refuge.'' The name means one who is protected by taking shelter beyond himself — fitting for a soul the Throne itself received."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', 'student:9ea3e355b4be564b', 1, 'is another name for love and for the fervent longing to seek the Belo…', 'is another name for love and for the fervent longing to seek the Beloved, which is ذَوْق', '<p>The apparent contradiction dissolves when we notice that the author is pointing to two angles of a single movement of the soul. Intense love (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>) is one — the consuming love that pulls a seeker toward the Beloved with every breath, leaving no room in the heart for anything else. Direct spiritual tasting (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَوْق</span>) is the other: <strong>the moment of unmediated encounter</strong>, like the tongue touching sweetness before the mind has any word for it.</p><p>When the author first says <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> is &quot;another name for the fervent longing... which is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَوْق</span>,&quot; he is not declaring them identical. He is saying they occupy the same ineffable register — and anyone who has experienced one is already moving inside the territory of the other. When he separates them as &quot;Love and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَوْق</span>,&quot; he acknowledges there is still <strong>a distinction worth naming</strong>. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> is the state of the seeker — the burning that makes a person reach. <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَوْق</span> is the state of the encounter — the direct experience of what was sought.</p><p>Think of someone whose love for music is so intense that the first note of a beloved piece brings them to tears — that is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span>. The sensation of the sound landing, that moment of direct experience, is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَوْق</span>. Yet <strong>neither can exist without the other</strong>, and neither can be explained to someone who has never heard music — which is why the author insists both are beyond speech and writing.</p><p>So <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عِشْق</span> and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذَوْق</span> are not two ranked stations on a ladder. They are <strong>two faces of a single spiritual reality</strong> — the orientation of the heart and its confirmation.</p>', '["عِشْق (intense love): root ع-ش-ق. The root conjures the عَاشِقَة — a clinging vine that fastens so tightly around whatever it grips that it cannot be pulled away. From this image comes عِشْق: love so total the lover clings entirely to the Beloved, unable to detach. Not ordinary affection, but the consuming attachment that leaves no space in the heart for anything else.", "ذَوْق (tasting, direct experience): root ذ-و-ق. From ذَاقَ, to taste — originally taking a small portion to know its quality by direct contact with the tongue. The Quran uses ذَآئِقَة in \"every soul shall taste death\" (Al-Imran 3:185), carrying the same quality of immediate, undeniable encounter. Spiritually, ذَوْق is the direct experience of a reality that cannot be transmitted to one who has not themselves tasted."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', 'student:cfcf28f0c05b7709', 2, 'I found in this one hadith the knowledge of all who came first and al…', 'I found in this one hadith the knowledge of all who came first and all who came last', '<p>The hadith looks, at first, like a simple formula for dividing our time and energy. But Shibli saw something deeper. <strong>The principle it contains is honest proportion</strong> — and when applied with full sincerity, it does not balance four competing claims; it reveals that three of them are almost nothing.</p><p>Consider the arithmetic the hadith forces upon us. How long will we live in this world? A few decades, perhaps. How long in the hereafter? Without end. So the effort we owe this world is a fraction of a fraction, and the effort we owe the hereafter is everything. Then comes the sharpest question: how much do we need Allah? Entirely. Without Him, nothing we are or have or do holds together for a single moment. And how much pain of the Fire can we bear? None. Followed faithfully, the hadith''s arithmetic collapses into a single direction: give everything toward Allah and what He has prescribed, and give this world only as much as it genuinely requires.</p><p>This is why Shibli could say he found in it the knowledge of all who came first and all who came last. Every prophet, every teacher, every tradition of guidance has ultimately been saying the same thing: <strong>know your real situation, and let that knowledge govern your effort</strong>. The Prophet (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ع</span>) himself sought refuge from knowledge that bears no fruit in action, because knowledge that does not reshape how we live is accumulation without transformation.</p><p>The passage connects this directly to <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَاهَدَة</span>, the disciplined inner struggle, because the proportional vision the hadith demands is itself that struggle. To sit quietly and reckon honestly — how long am I here, how long is forever, how much do I need Him — and then to let that reckoning cut away the excess, is exactly what <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">مُجَاهَدَة</span> looks like in practice. Shibli''s single hadith was not a shortcut; <strong>it was the one key that unlocks every door</strong>.</p>', '["قَدْر (measure, proportion): from the root ق-د-ر, meaning to measure, to decree, to be capable. The same root gives قَدَر (divine decree) and قُدْرَة (power, ability). In the hadith, بِقَدْرِ means ''in proportion to'' — the word carries the sense that true measure is already written into the nature of a thing, and our effort must align with that reality.", "مُجَاهَدَة (disciplined inner struggle): from the root ج-ه-د, meaning to exert, to strive, to push to one''s limit. The same root gives جِهَاد and اِجْتِهَاد (independent reasoning by effort). The form مُفَاعَلَة implies a sustained, ongoing engagement — مُجَاهَدَة is not a single push but a continuing discipline, a daily wrestling with the demands of the lower self."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', 'student:ea8cecb3ef5dcef4', 1, 'I disciplined it so thoroughly that I refused to satisfy any of its c…', 'I disciplined it so thoroughly that I refused to satisfy any of its cravings, until at last it found its peace and its contentment in the worship of Allah', '<p>Think of the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> as something like an appetite — not evil by nature, but restless, always reaching for whatever promises satisfaction next. Left to itself, it chases sensation, comfort, and desire the way a fire chases fuel: the more it is fed, the louder it demands. <strong>This restlessness is not a flaw to be destroyed but a force to be redirected</strong> — which is why Hatim''s choice to oppose his <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> was not an act of annihilation but a deliberate turning of energy from one channel to another.</p><p>Every time we refuse a craving of the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span>, we are withdrawing nourishment from one direction and forcing the self to look elsewhere. The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> does not die from refusal. <strong>Sustained refusal retrains the appetite itself.</strong> Just as a person who gives up hollow pleasures long enough finds that simpler, deeper things begin to satisfy — that what once seemed bland now carries real sweetness — the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> deprived of its old diet of distraction gradually discovers that worship offers a depth it was always seeking in the wrong places.</p><p>Hatim does not describe this as a struggle that ended through gritted teeth. He describes something more complete: the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> found its peace and contentment in the worship of Allah. The transformation he names is the Quranic promise of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النفس المطمئنة</span> — the soul at rest — not a soul that has been silenced but one that has <strong>arrived at what it was always searching for.</strong></p><p>What makes this real rather than mere willpower is time and repetition. Discipline held across days and years does not merely suppress desire — it reshapes what desire reaches for. The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> must move toward something. The seeker''s task is to ensure that, over and over, <strong>the only movement available is toward Allah</strong> — until that movement itself becomes its own peace.</p>', '["نَفْس: from the root ن-ف-س, meaning to breathe. The root gives نَفَس (a breath) and تَنَفُّس (respiration). From breath it names the full living, appetitive self — the creature that inhales and exhales and, in doing so, longs and desires. The نَفْس is not a soul in the abstract but the breathing being whose very life is motion toward something."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', 'student:c7290629e231db3c', 2, 'all four of these Books of God turn upon these very eight benefits. W…', 'all four of these Books of God turn upon these very eight benefits. Whoever acts upon these eight principles is as one who has acted upon all four holy Books', '<p>The question cuts to the heart of what scripture is for. Every one of the four Books — the Tawrat, the Injil, the Zabur, and the Quran — was revealed to a specific people, in a specific tongue, in a specific age, and so each carries its own laws, its own stories, its own warnings. Yet beneath that vast diversity of language and law, all four are answering the same question: what is wrong with the human being, and how does it get fixed?</p><p>Think of it this way. A master builder might fill an entire library with architectural plans — for houses, bridges, towers, and foundations. The drawings are all different, but every single one is governed by the same handful of physical laws: gravity, tension, load, and balance. <strong>Hatim understood the laws.</strong> The eight resolutions are not a summary of scripture''s contents; they are a map of its purpose — the handful of root conditions in the human soul that all four Books were sent to cure.</p><p>The first three resolutions address the soul''s relationship with what is passing. Making righteous deeds our only true beloved strikes at the root error all four Books warn against: loving the world more than what endures. Opposing the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَفْس</span> and its cravings is the inner battle every prophet called their people to fight. And releasing wealth to Allah by giving it to the poor is the lived expression of the Quranic truth that nothing we cling to here is actually ours.</p><p>The next three turn outward. Seeking honor through <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَقْوَى</span> alone — not tribe, not wealth, not power — dismantles the pride that has driven human beings to ruin since before any scripture was written. Accepting that all provision is apportioned by Allah extinguishes envy at its root. And taking Shaytan as the only enemy dissolves enmity between people, because the true adversary was never another human being.</p><p>The final two resolutions are the seal. A soul that trusts Allah for its sustenance and places all reliance upon Him through <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">توَكُّل</span> has nothing left to fear, nothing left to grasp, and nothing left to prove.</p><p>Shaykh Shafeeq''s point is this: the Books are long because we need stories, warnings, and laws repeated in a hundred ways before our hearts yield. Hatim had absorbed the destination. And when we reach the destination, we have walked every road that leads there.</p>', '["نَفْس (self): from the root ن-ف-س, meaning to breathe. The breath is the most intimate, most continuous act of a living being — and so نَفْس came to name the self at its deepest core: that breathing center of desire, will, and consciousness that every prophet called us to govern.", "تَقْوَى (God-consciousness): from the root و-ق-ى, meaning to protect or shield. The image is of placing something between yourself and harm. تَقْوَى is the inner shield a person builds through constant awareness of Allah — a guard that turns the soul away from error before it even approaches.", "توَكُّل (reliance): from the root و-ك-ل, meaning to entrust or hand over to another''s care. When we وَكَّل someone, we appoint them as our agent and step back entirely. توَكُّل on Allah is exactly that — stepping back from our own schemes and handing every affair to the One who is sufficient.", "اَلتَّوْفِيق (enabling grace): from the root و-ف-ق, meaning to accord or align. The root carries the sense of two things meeting in harmony. اَلتَّوْفِيق is when Allah aligns a person''s inner will and outer capacity with what is right — not merely permission, but the actual enabling to do it."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'finding a true guide', 'student:46fa29837876b2a0', 1, 'Such spiritual guides are exceedingly hard to find. But if a man has…', 'Such spiritual guides are exceedingly hard to find. But if a man has been granted this wealth and blessing, so that he has found such a guide', '<p>The passage does not leave the seeker without tools — it embeds the answer inside its very description of the true guide. <strong>The first and clearest test is legal</strong>: a true <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شيخ الكامل</span> will never instruct a disciple to do anything forbidden by the Shariah. The impostors, the text warns, do exactly that — they command what Allah has forbidden under the cover of spiritual authority. If a guide''s instruction crosses that line, the disciple has been shown everything he needs to know.</p><p>Beyond that hard line, the passage hands the seeker <strong>a living portrait</strong>: a man who has visibly turned away from love of the world, of status, and of material gain — not in words but in his manner of living. He eats little, sleeps little, prays abundantly, and gives in charity. His character carries patience, humility, contentment, and truthfulness in a way that is observable over time, not merely claimed at a first meeting. These are qualities that compound; a counterfeit wears thin.</p><p>The chain itself is a test. A true guide has taken <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البيعة</span> upon the hand of his own perfected guide, and that guide upon the hand of his, link by link back to the Prophet Muhammad. <strong>This lineage is not a credential that can be invented</strong>; it is a living transmission, and the seeker can ask about it plainly. An impostor either cannot name the chain or names one whose links do not hold.</p><p>The <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرياضة</span> and <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المجاهدة</span> — the disciplines and inner struggles the guide must himself have passed through — leave their marks in a person. A man who has genuinely wrestled with his <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span> and brought it to heel carries a stillness, <strong>a freedom from anger, jealousy, and greed</strong>, that is felt in his presence rather than announced by his tongue. The seeker who is honest with himself will know the difference between performance and the real thing.</p>', '["شيخ الكامل (perfected spiritual guide): شيخ comes from the root ش-ي-خ, evoking age, whiteness of hair, and the dignity earned through long experience. كامل comes from ك-م-ل, meaning to be whole or complete — a كامل is one in whom nothing essential is lacking. Together the phrase names a man fully ripened, not merely learned.", "البيعة (solemn covenant): from ب-ي-ع, the root for buying and selling — a transaction in which both parties give something. In البيعة the disciple offers his will and the guide offers his hand and his care; it is an exchange of trust, not a one-sided submission. The market imagery is deliberate: both sides are bound.", "الرياضة (spiritual discipline): from ر-و-ض, meaning to tame or break in — the same root used for training an unbroken horse. روضة is also a garden, made cultivatable by patient, repeated tending. الرياضة is the taming of one''s own nature, turning wildness into something that yields fruit.", "المجاهدة (inner striving): from ج-ه-د, the root of exertion and effort, the same family as جهاد. المجاهدة is the interior version: the sustained, deliberate struggle against whatever within the self resists Allah — fought not on a battlefield but inside the heart.", "نفس (lower self): from ن-ف-س, the root connected to breath — نفس also means a single inhalation. The نفس is literally what animates a person, the breathing self. In this context it is the commanding soul, the one that breathes desire and appetite into the heart and whispers the seeker away from Allah."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'finding a true guide', 'student:a435aabf9e55f343', 2, 'He should not perform lengthy supererogatory prayers before the Shayk…', 'He should not perform lengthy supererogatory prayers before the Shaykh after each obligatory prayer', '<p>The disciple who lingers in extended voluntary prayers before his guide is not simply being devout — he is <strong>performing for an audience</strong>. The lower self (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نفس</span>) is subtle in its deceptions: what looks like worship can mask a bid for the Shaykh''s admiration, a quiet signal that one is serious, committed, and spiritually advanced. This is the trap the etiquette is designed to spring.</p><p>The time spent in a guide''s presence is not like ordinary time. Something passes in those moments of attentive stillness that cannot pass while the disciple is turned inward in personal devotion. <strong>The posture of surplus prayer before the Shaykh is a posture of independence</strong> — choosing one''s own spiritual rhythm over the guide''s, and that breaks the very surrender that makes the relationship transformative.</p><p>The true guide has already passed through every form of spiritual discipline (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الرياضة</span>) and inner struggle (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">المجاهدة</span>). He knows what the disciple needs and when. To pile up voluntary prayers (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نوافل</span>) in his presence is to say he knows better — and that is the pride the Path is meant to dissolve. The outward etiquettes are not bureaucratic rules; they are a mirror in which the ego can be caught at its oldest game, dressing itself in the clothing of religion.</p>', '["نوافل (voluntary prayers): from the root ن-ف-ل, which carries the sense of giving beyond what is owed — a surplus freely given. A نافلة is therefore a prayer performed not because it is required but as an act of devotion above the obligatory duty. The root illuminates why the etiquette matters: what is meant as a gift to Allah can become, in the wrong moment, a display aimed at a human audience.", "نفس (lower self): from the root ن-ف-س, meaning to breathe. The breath is both what sustains life and what reveals hidden agitation — the نفس is the breathing, desiring, restless interior that must be trained through discipline rather than simply silenced. Its very name ties the self to breath, suggesting how intimate and involuntary its workings are.", "الرياضة (spiritual discipline): from the root ر-و-ض, evoking a meadow or pasture where an animal is patiently broken in and trained. The root connects the taming of a creature to the cultivation of a field — productive, careful work, not punishment. What the farmer does to unruly ground, الرياضة does to the soul."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', 'student:6ed2b2781fd47c83', 1, 'some are such that to write their answers is not permitted', 'some are such that to write their answers is not permitted', '<p>There is a distinction at the heart of every genuine spiritual tradition, and al-Ghazali names it plainly here. Some knowledge can be set down in ink — it describes obligations, maps the outward road, clarifies what is required. But there is another kind that <strong>writing cannot carry</strong>: the inner reality, <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الباطن</span>, which does not explain but transforms, and transforms only through direct encounter, teacher to student, when the student is ready to receive.</p><p>The authority that forbids writing is not a human institution — it is the nature of the knowledge itself. To commit these inner realities to paper risks two things:</p><ul><li><p>The unprepared reader mistakes the description for the thing itself, and stops traveling</p></li><li><p>The words, stripped of their living context, become a cage rather than a door</p></li></ul><p>This is why the passage turns immediately to the story of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خضر</span> and Musa — even a prophet was told not to ask until his teacher chose the moment to speak.</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلاَ تَسْئَلْنِي عَنْ شَيْءٍ حَتَّى أُحْدِثَ لَكَ مِنْهُ ذِكْرًا</span> &quot;Do not question me about anything until I myself bring it up to you and explain it.&quot; Al-Kahf 18:70</p><p>The teacher <strong>protects</strong>. Knowledge given before its time does not illuminate; it confuses, and sometimes destroys. The seeker''s capacity must grow to meet the knowledge, not the other way around. Act upon what you already know, and through that acting, what you do not yet know will be unveiled. The road itself is the teacher — and it only speaks to those already walking it.</p>', '["الباطن (the inner, the hidden): from the root ب-ط-ن, meaning the belly or interior of a thing. Just as the belly holds nourishment before the body is ready to use it, الباطن is the inward face of reality — the living truth concealed beneath the outward form, waiting until the soul matures enough to receive it without harm.", "خضر (the Evergreen One): from the root خ-ض-ر, meaning to be green, fresh, and perpetually verdant. الخضر — the teacher who never withers — carries his wisdom in his very name: living knowledge that cannot be pressed between the pages of a book, always renewed, always timed to the readiness of the one who stands before him."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', 'student:ca121916fbde5247', 2, 'do not ask me about what is difficult for you except through the tong…', 'do not ask me about what is difficult for you except through the tongue of the heart', '<p>The tongue of the heart (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">لسان القلب</span>) is not a quiet prayer or an inward whisper. It is the soul''s own readiness — the genuine need that forms inside a seeker only after he has walked far enough along the road to receive what he is reaching for.</p><p>When the teacher says &quot;ask me only through the tongue of the heart,&quot; he is drawing a line between two kinds of questioning. A verbal question can arrive from anywhere — curiosity, impatience, the wish to seem eager. The heart''s question cannot be manufactured; it only takes shape once the soul has prepared itself through obedience, contentment, and sincerity (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِخْلَاص</span>).</p><p>So how does a student actually use it? Not by sitting still and thinking harder. The three Quranic verses the teacher quotes all give the same method: wait with patience, do not hasten, travel through the earth and see. The student works, endures, stays within the Shariah, and as that practice deepens him, what he needs to know surfaces on its own.</p><p>The difference from asking in words is a difference of <strong>source</strong>. A word-question comes from the mind; it can arrive before the student is ready, it can be driven by <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رِيَاء</span>, and it pulls the teacher away from the right moment. The heart-question arises from the soul''s actual condition. Khidr''s instruction — &quot;do not question me about anything until I myself bring it up&quot; — is exactly this: right knowledge comes at the right moment, and forcing it with words breaks the timing.</p><p>At its root, the tongue of the heart is tawakkul (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَوَكُّل</span>) applied to learning — the trust that what Allah has written for you will reach you, and that what He has not written will not come no matter how urgently you ask.</p>', '["لسان (tongue): from the root ل-س-ن, linked to the organ of speech and to language itself. The root carries a sense of fluency and moisture — the tongue as what gives inner meaning an outward shape. When the teacher speaks of the لسان القلب, he is saying: let the heart speak with its own native voice, not borrowed words.", "قلب (heart): from ق-ل-ب, meaning to flip or turn something over. The heart is named for its constant turning — between states of fear and hope, contraction and openness. It is never fixed, which is why its language is a live orientation rather than a fixed question.", "تَوَكُّل (reliance on Allah): from و-ك-ل, to delegate or entrust. The تَفَعُّل pattern makes the act reflexive and intensive — you actively hand yourself over. Not passive resignation, but the deliberate placing of your affairs in Allah''s hands.", "إِخْلَاص (sincerity): from خ-ل-ص, to be freed from mixture, to be refined. Like purifying metal by removing alloy. إِخْلَاص is the state of an act with no foreign motive remaining — intention directed to Allah alone, unmixed.", "رِيَاء (ostentation): from ر-أ-ي, to see. رِيَاء is the act of making yourself seen — performing for an audience. The root exposes the disease: an action infected by the gaze of others, when it should exist only before Allah."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', 'student:0c8eaee9664bcafa', 1, 'the corruption that this speaker works in their religion, not even th…', 'the corruption that this speaker works in their religion, not even the devil can work its like', '<p>The passage identifies a specific kind of spiritual danger that is worse than ordinary temptation — not because it is more powerful, but because it is <strong>better disguised</strong>. The devil tempts people toward what they already suspect is wrong; their conscience can at least name what is happening. But the false preacher — the one who mounts the pulpit seeking applause, moved only by the desire that people weep loudly and tear their garments — does his damage while wearing the face of religion itself. The audience walks in seeking a cure and walks out having been administered more of the disease.</p><p>The mechanism works through substitution. True preaching is meant to make the fires of the Hereafter real in the listener''s chest — to open their eyes to their own negligence and drive them from their rest. But the applause-seeking preacher gives the audience an emotion instead of an awakening. The crowd weeps together, the garments are torn, and everyone says &quot;what an excellent gathering this was&quot; — and they leave exactly as they came. <strong>That feeling of satisfaction is the injury.</strong> A person who knows they are sick will seek a doctor; a person made to feel well when they are not will seek no one.</p><p>What makes this exceed even the devil''s reach is scope and repetition. The devil works on one soul at a time. The preacher works on a crowd, week after week, using the very platform appointed for their guidance as the instrument of their confirmation in heedlessness. The text draws a sharp conclusion: whoever holds authority is obliged, as part of enjoining the right (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأمر بالمعروف</span>) and forbidding the wrong (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النهي عن المنكر</span>), to remove such a speaker from the pulpit — not as a matter of opinion, but as a religious duty — because what is being corrupted is <strong>the mechanism of repair itself.</strong></p>', NULL);
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', 'student:67e6a74052d9a2db', 2, 'would set that aside for some of his chambers', 'would set that aside for some of his chambers', '<p>The word rendered &quot;chambers&quot; translates the Arabic <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُجَر</span> — the plural of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُجرة</span>, a small enclosed room. These were the literal rooms built along the outer wall of the Prophet''s mosque in Medina, one for each wife. Classical Arabic lets the dwelling stand for its dweller; to name the chamber was to name the woman who lived in it.</p><p>The shift to &quot;her who was a possessor of certainty&quot; resolves a distinction al-Ghazali plants in the very sentence before. Among the wives, some carried a recognizable human softness: a need for the security of stored provision. The Prophet honored that need by meeting it, setting aside a year''s worth for them. But there was <strong>another kind of wife entirely</strong>: one whose <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يقين</span> was so rooted in Allah that storing grain for her would have been an insult to the trust she already lived from. For her, he set aside only a day''s provision, or half of it — the precise minimum, matched to a soul that had no use for more.</p><p>The author never names her because the name is beside the point. al-Ghazali is not writing biography; he is writing a law of the inner life. <strong>The unnamed wife is a living proof</strong> that the provision a soul truly needs from this world moves exactly opposite to the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يقين</span> held in her heart — the deeper the certainty, the thinner the dependence on worldly store. She is not a historical figure to be identified but a measure of our own reliance on Allah.</p>', '["حُجَر (chambers): from the root ح-ج-ر, meaning to enclose, confine, or hold firm — as a stone arrests movement or a wall closes a space. حُجرة is the enclosed room itself. The wives of the Prophet each occupied one such room beside his mosque; classical Arabic named the woman by naming the space she inhabited.", "يقين (certainty): from the root ي-ق-ن, meaning to become still and settled, free of all wavering. يقين is not a firmly held opinion — it is the condition of a heart that has stopped shifting. The wife this passage describes lived from that stillness; she did not need the world to compensate for what her heart already held."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', 'student:b90283d83dd32e06', 3, 'As for her who was a possessor of certainty, he would set aside for h…', 'As for her who was a possessor of certainty, he would set aside for her no more than the provision of a day, or half of it', '<p>The year''s provision is not the destination — it is a kindness extended to those who are not yet ready to release it entirely. Al-Ghazali makes this plain by showing how the Prophet distributed what he set aside: some received a year''s worth, but only because he saw a weakness in their hearts. Those who had already arrived at certainty (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يقين</span>) received a day''s provision, or half of one.</p><p>What separates the two groups is not virtue in the ordinary sense — it is certainty. A heart that is certain does not need a storeroom to feel safe. It knows, without argument, that tomorrow belongs to Allah, and that worrying over tomorrow''s bread is a quiet form of disbelief in His care. The person who holds onto a year''s supply is not doing wrong; he is simply still learning to let go.</p><p>So this counsel is a ladder. The reader is placed on the first rung — do not accumulate more than a year — because that is where most of us honestly stand. By showing the Prophet''s own practice with those who possessed certainty, it points the reader toward what real trust looks like: not a moderate stockpile, but a hand open enough to hold only today. <strong>The year is a concession, and concessions are invitations to outgrow them.</strong></p><p>The reader is not told to feel guilty for needing the full year''s provision. But he is told to notice the difference between needing it and resting in it, and to understand that spiritual growth moves him toward the shorter window. The Prophet''s prayer — that the provision of his family be <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كَفَاف</span>, just enough — was not a prayer for comfort. It was a prayer for freedom.</p>', '["يقين (certainty): from the root ي-ق-ن, meaning to be firm and settled. The root carries the sense of something that no longer shifts — a knowing that has stopped moving. يقين is not confidence; it is the stillness that remains when doubt has been completely expelled from the heart.", "كفاف (just enough): from the root ك-ف-ف, meaning to hold back or restrain — the same root as كف, the palm of the hand, which holds without gripping. كفاف names the exact measure that neither falls short nor spills over: provision that rescues you from both hunger and hoarding."]');
INSERT INTO source_reference (slug, anchor_key, page_range, headings) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'knowledge that will not save you', 'pp. 4-6', '["IMAM GHAZALI''S RESPONSE TO THE LETTER", "In the Name of Allah (God), the Most Compassionate and the Most Merciful.", "My dear beloved son and true friend!", "The Holy Prophet Muhammad (Peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has furthermore said", "Shaykh Junaid Baghdadi (RA) replied", "And at another place the Exalted Allah has said"]');
INSERT INTO source_reference (slug, anchor_key, page_range, headings) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'the striving that mercy meets', 'pp. 6-8', '["Upon hearing this message from the angel, the righteous man replied", "The angel then returned to the Presence of Allah, the Exalted, and said", "Upon this, Allah, the Exalted, said", "My dear beloved son! Hear what the Messenger of Allah, Muhammad (Peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), says", "Ali (May Allah honor his countenance) said", "Shaykh Hasan al-Basri (May Allah shower His Mercy upon him) says", "Another intimate of Allah says", "A prophetic tradition (*hadith*) says"]');
INSERT INTO source_reference (slug, anchor_key, page_range, headings) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'the hours before dawn', 'pp. 8-12', '["My dear beloved son! Every day the Voice of Allah resonates within your heart", "And it will be said to you", "But God forbid that you should be among the animals, of whom it has been said", "On another occasion, the Messenger of Allah (may peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a Companion", "In this verse, there is an expression of divine gratitude toward such servants.", "In this verse, there is an honorable mention of such servants of Allah.", "The Messenger of Allah (may peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), master of both worlds, said", "Shaykh Sufyan al-Thawri (may Allah shower His mercy upon him), one of the intimate friends of Allah, said"]');
INSERT INTO source_reference (slug, anchor_key, page_range, headings) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', 'pp. 12-14', '[]');
INSERT INTO source_reference (slug, anchor_key, page_range, headings) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', 'pp. 14-19', '["Shaykh Shafeeq al-Balkhi, may Allah shower His mercy upon him, said", "BENEFITS NARRATED BY HATIM BIN ISM, MAY ALLAH BE PLEASED WITH HIM", "Therefore I adopted complete reliance upon Allah in every matter.\""]');
INSERT INTO source_reference (slug, anchor_key, page_range, headings) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'finding a true guide', 'pp. 19-21', '["My dear beloved son! You now know from these traditions that you do not need a great quantity of knowledge.", "QUALITIES OF A SPIRITUAL GUIDE (SHAYKH)", "OBEDIENCE TO A SPIRITUAL GUIDE", "OUTER ETIQUETTES (RESPECT)", "INNER ETIQUETTES (RESPECT)"]');
INSERT INTO source_reference (slug, anchor_key, page_range, headings) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', 'pp. 21-23', '["REALITY OF TASAWWUF (IHSAN, SUFISM, SPIRITUAL STRUGGLE)", "REALITY OF SERVITUDE TO GOD", "REALITY OF RELIANCE ON GOD (TAWAKKUL)", "REALITY OF SINCERITY TO GOD (IKHLAS)", "Accept the counsel of Khidr (peace be upon him)", "Do not be hasty. When the time comes, you will be told and shown.", "Shaykh Zun Noon Misri (may Allah shower His mercy upon him) said something deeply instructive to one of his students"]');
INSERT INTO source_reference (slug, anchor_key, page_range, headings) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', 'pp. 23-29', '["My dear beloved son! I will now bring my explanation to a close with eight admonitions.", "EIGHT ADMONITIONS", "The remedy, then, is to leave such a jealous person to remain afflicted with their disease", "Four Matters Worthy of Our Actions"]');
INSERT INTO source_reference (slug, anchor_key, page_range, headings) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'a prayer for the road', 'pp. 29-30', '["A SUPPLICATION THAT MAY ALWAYS BE RECITED", "This book comes to an end with the help of Al-Malik, the Absolute Sovereign, and Al-Wahhab, the Giver of all."]');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'blurb', NULL, 'Ayyuha al-Walad', 0, NULL, 'This is a letter of spiritual counsel. A former student, having spent years mastering the religious sciences under his teacher, asked what, of all he had learned, would truly serve him in the grave and on the Day of Judgment. Everything that follows is the teacher''s written reply. The title, Ayyuha al-Walad — "O son" — is the affectionate way he addresses the young man throughout; it names a beloved disciple, not a child of his body.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad', 'this is a letter of spiritual counsel a former student having spent years mastering the religious sciences under his teacher asked what of all he had learned would truly serve him in the grave and on the day of judgment everything that follows is the teachers written reply the title ayyuha al walad o son is the affectionate way he addresses the young man throughout it names a beloved disciple not a child of his body', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'introduction to the book', 'Introduction to the Book', 0, NULL, 'This is a letter of spiritual counsel. A former student, having spent years mastering the religious sciences under his teacher, asked what, of all he had learned, would truly serve him in the grave and on the Day of Judgment. Everything that follows is the teacher''s written reply. The title, Ayyuha al-Walad — "O son" — is the affectionate way he addresses the young man throughout; it names a beloved disciple, not a child of his body.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad introduction to the book', 'this is a letter of spiritual counsel a former student having spent years mastering the religious sciences under his teacher asked what of all he had learned would truly serve him in the grave and on the day of judgment everything that follows is the teachers written reply the title ayyuha al walad o son is the affectionate way he addresses the young man throughout it names a beloved disciple not a child of his body', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'introduction to the book', 'Introduction to the Book', 1, NULL, 'It is written in the first person. The "I" is the author himself, speaking directly to the one student he answers, with no narrator standing between them. The author is Imam Ghazali, who names several of his own larger works — the Ihya al-Ulum ad-Din, the Kimiya as-Saada, the Jawahir ul-Quran — for a reader who wants fuller treatment. One point rewards attention: the letter urges the seeker to go out and find a living guide of his own, and the sixth chapter is given over to recognizing a true one. That guide is a figure distinct from the author writing here, who was already the student''s own teacher.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad introduction to the book', 'it is written in the first person the i is the author himself speaking directly to the one student he answers with no narrator standing between them the author is imam ghazali who names several of his own larger works the ihya al ulum ad din the kimiya as saada the jawahir ul quran for a reader who wants fuller treatment one point rewards attention the letter urges the seeker to go out and find a living guide of his own and the sixth chapter is given over to recognizing a true one that guide is a figure distinct from the author writing here who was already the students own teacher', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'introduction to the book', 'Introduction to the Book', 2, NULL, 'The chapters trace a single argument: knowledge that does not change how one lives will not save its owner, and what does is striving, the prayer of the hours before dawn, and worship understood as plain obedience. Verses of scripture and sayings of the Prophet are quoted in Arabic and then unfolded. It closes in a prayer for the road ahead.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad introduction to the book', 'the chapters trace a single argument knowledge that does not change how one lives will not save its owner and what does is striving the prayer of the hours before dawn and worship understood as plain obedience verses of scripture and sayings of the prophet are quoted in arabic and then unfolded it closes in a prayer for the road ahead', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 0, NULL, 'Know that one of Imam Ghazali''s (رَضِيَ اللّٰهُ عَنْه) foremost students had remained in his service for a number of years, studying under him and drawing deeply from his knowledge and his spiritual guidance. In that time he mastered the finer points of the sciences and refined the virtues of his soul. Then one day he began to reflect on his own state. A question pressed upon his heart, and he said within himself: I have studied many branches of knowledge. I have given the prime of my life to learning them and gathering them, and I have sacrificed a great deal to do so. Now I ought to know which of these sciences will benefit me tomorrow and keep me company in my grave and come to my aid on the Day of Judgment, and which of them will not benefit me on that Day, so that I may set it aside. For the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'know that one of imam ghazalis رضي الله عنه foremost students had remained in his service for a number of years studying under him and drawing deeply from his knowledge and his spiritual guidance in that time he mastered the finer points of the sciences and refined the virtues of his soul then one day he began to reflect on his own state a question pressed upon his heart and he said within himself i have studied many branches of knowledge i have given the prime of my life to learning them and gathering them and i have sacrificed a great deal to do so now i ought to know which of these sciences will benefit me tomorrow and keep me company in my grave and come to my aid on the day of judgment and which of them will not benefit me on that day so that i may set it aside for the messenger of allah may allah bless him and grant him peace said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 1, NULL, 'Prophetic traditionاَللَّهُمَّ أَعُوذُ بِكَ مِنْ عِلْمٍ لَا يَنْفَعُ"O Allah, I seek refuge in You from knowledge that does not benefit."', '', NULL, 'Prophetic tradition', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'prophetic traditionاللهم اعوذ بك من علم لا ينفع o allah i seek refuge in you from knowledge that does not benefit', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 2, NULL, 'This thought stayed with him for several days. At last he wrote a letter to Imam Ghazali (رض), asking for an answer to what troubled him, together with replies to certain other questions. He asked as well for personal counsel, and for a supplication he might recite at all times.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'this thought stayed with him for several days at last he wrote a letter to imam ghazali رض asking for an answer to what troubled him together with replies to certain other questions he asked as well for personal counsel and for a supplication he might recite at all times', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 3, NULL, 'In his letter he acknowledged that Imam Ghazali (رض) had already written many books on these very subjects, among them Revival of the Knowledge of the Path to God, Alchemy of Ultimate Success and Happiness, Priceless Gems of the Holy Quran, Forty Steps, and The Best Way for the Worshippers of God Leading Them to the Paradise of the Lord of All Universes, and others besides. Yet this humble one, he wrote, needed something he could study without pause and act upon without fail.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'in his letter he acknowledged that imam ghazali رض had already written many books on these very subjects among them revival of the knowledge of the path to god alchemy of ultimate success and happiness priceless gems of the holy quran forty steps and the best way for the worshippers of god leading them to the paradise of the lord of all universes and others besides yet this humble one he wrote needed something he could study without pause and act upon without fail', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 4, NULL, 'In answer to his letter, Imam Ghazali (رض) sent him the counsel that follows.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'in answer to his letter imam ghazali رض sent him the counsel that follows', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 5, NULL, 'In the Name of Allah, the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful. All praise belongs to Allah alone, the Lord of all the worlds, and may the blessings and peace of Allah be upon His Messenger Muhammad and upon all of his descendants and his followers.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'in the name of allah the most compassionate the most merciful all praise belongs to allah alone the lord of all the worlds and may the blessings and peace of allah be upon his messenger muhammad and upon all of his descendants and his followers', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 6, NULL, 'My dear beloved son and true friend, may Allah the Exalted grant you a long life spent in His worship and obedience, and may He set you upon the path of His beloved ones. Know that the wellspring of all good counsel is the holy person of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, for every counsel is founded upon his sayings and his way, upon his outward conduct and his inward states alike. Any counsel that runs against his sayings and his way carries no benefit at all. Countless words of advice have already been written and passed down in the light of his sayings and his way. If they have guided you, then you have no need of any counsel from me. But if you have drawn no benefit from the counsels of the Prophet ﷺ, then tell me: in all the years now gone, what knowledge have you truly gained from me?', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'my dear beloved son and true friend may allah the exalted grant you a long life spent in his worship and obedience and may he set you upon the path of his beloved ones know that the wellspring of all good counsel is the holy person of the prophet muhammad ﷺ for every counsel is founded upon his sayings and his way upon his outward conduct and his inward states alike any counsel that runs against his sayings and his way carries no benefit at all countless words of advice have already been written and passed down in the light of his sayings and his way if they have guided you then you have no need of any counsel from me but if you have drawn no benefit from the counsels of the prophet ﷺ then tell me in all the years now gone what knowledge have you truly gained from me', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 7, NULL, 'My dear beloved son, among the counsels the Prophet ﷺ gave to his community is this saying of his:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'my dear beloved son among the counsels the prophet ﷺ gave to his community is this saying of his', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 8, NULL, 'Sayingعَلَامَةُ إِعْرَاضِ اللَّهِ تَعَالَىٰ عَنِ الْعَبْدِ اشْتِغَالُهُ بِمَا لَا يَعْنِيهِ، وَ إِنَّ امْرَأً ذَهَبَتْ سَاعَةٌ مِنْ عُمْرِهِ فِي غَيْرِ مَا خُلِقَ لَهُ مِنَ الْعِبَادَةِ، جدير ان تطول عليه حسرته"A servant''s busying himself with matters that do not concern him is a sign that Allah has turned His special favor away from him. And if a single hour of a servant''s life passes in the pursuit of anything other than that for which Allah created him, then it is fitting that his sorrow over it should be long."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'sayingعلامه اعراض الله تعالي عن العبد اشتغاله بما لا يعنيه و ان امرا ذهبت ساعه من عمره في غير ما خلق له من العباده جدير ان تطول عليه حسرته a servants busying himself with matters that do not concern him is a sign that allah has turned his special favor away from him and if a single hour of a servants life passes in the pursuit of anything other than that for which allah created him then it is fitting that his sorrow over it should be long', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 9, NULL, 'The Prophet ﷺ has also said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'the prophet ﷺ has also said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 10, NULL, 'Prophetic traditionمَنْ جَاوَزَ الْأَرْبَعِينَ وَلَمْ يَغْلِبْ خَيْرُهُ عَلَىٰ شَرِّهِ فَلْيَتَجَهَّزْ إِلَىٰ النَّارِ"Whoever passes forty years of age and the good within him still does not prevail over the evil, let him prepare himself for the Fire."', '', NULL, 'Prophetic tradition', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'prophetic traditionمن جاوز الاربعين ولم يغلب خيره علي شره فليتجهز الي النار whoever passes forty years of age and the good within him still does not prevail over the evil let him prepare himself for the fire', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 11, NULL, 'In this counsel there is enough for the people of knowledge.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'in this counsel there is enough for the people of knowledge', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 12, NULL, 'My dear beloved son, giving such advice is easy, but accepting it is hard, for to the taste of those who follow their desires it is bitter, since forbidden things are dear to their hearts. This is all the more true of the one who seeks formal learning, busy with the advancement of his own self and with the distinctions of this world. Such a man supposes that bare knowledge alone will be his salvation and his deliverance, and that he has no need of action. This is the belief of the philosophers. Glory be to Allah the Mighty. This deluded one does not even see that once he has gained knowledge, if he does not act upon it, the proof against him only stands the firmer. Does he not know that the Prophet ﷺ said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'my dear beloved son giving such advice is easy but accepting it is hard for to the taste of those who follow their desires it is bitter since forbidden things are dear to their hearts this is all the more true of the one who seeks formal learning busy with the advancement of his own self and with the distinctions of this world such a man supposes that bare knowledge alone will be his salvation and his deliverance and that he has no need of action this is the belief of the philosophers glory be to allah the mighty this deluded one does not even see that once he has gained knowledge if he does not act upon it the proof against him only stands the firmer does he not know that the prophet ﷺ said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 13, NULL, 'Prophetic traditionأَشَدُّ النَّاسِ عَذَابًا يَوْمَ الْقِيَامَةِ عَالِمٌ لَا يَنْفَعُهُ اللَّهُ بِعِلْمِهِ"The most severely punished of people on the Day of Resurrection is a scholar whom Allah did not benefit through his knowledge."', '', NULL, 'Prophetic tradition', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'prophetic traditionاشد الناس عذابا يوم القيامه عالم لا ينفعه الله بعلمه the most severely punished of people on the day of resurrection is a scholar whom allah did not benefit through his knowledge', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 14, NULL, 'It is related that Shaykh Junaid al-Baghdadi (رض) was seen in a dream after his death, and he was asked, "O Abu al-Qasim, what news do you bring of your state?" He replied:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'it is related that shaykh junaid al baghdadi رض was seen in a dream after his death and he was asked o abu al qasim what news do you bring of your state he replied', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 15, NULL, 'Sayingطَاحَتْ تِلْكَ الْعِبَارَاتُ، وَ فَنِيَتْ تِلْكَ الْإِشَارَاتُ، وَ مَا نَفَعَنَا إِلَّا رُكَيْعَاتٌ رَكَعْنَاهَا فِي جَوْفِ اللَّيْلِ"Those fine expressions came to nothing, and those subtle allusions perished, and nothing profited us except a few humble cycles of prayer that we prayed in the depth of the night."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'sayingطاحت تلك العبارات و فنيت تلك الاشارات و ما نفعنا الا ركيعات ركعناها في جوف الليل those fine expressions came to nothing and those subtle allusions perished and nothing profited us except a few humble cycles of prayer that we prayed in the depth of the night', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 16, NULL, 'My dear beloved son, do not be bankrupt of good deeds, and do not be empty of inner states, and be certain that bare knowledge will not take you by the hand. Consider its likeness. Suppose a man in the wilderness held ten Indian swords along with other weapons, and suppose he were brave and a man of war, and then a great and terrifying lion charged at him. What do you think? Would the weapons turn its harm away without his drawing them and striking? It is plain that they turn nothing away except by being wielded and by striking. In just this way, were a man to read and learn a hundred thousand points of knowledge and yet not act upon them, they would profit him nothing except through action.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'my dear beloved son do not be bankrupt of good deeds and do not be empty of inner states and be certain that bare knowledge will not take you by the hand consider its likeness suppose a man in the wilderness held ten indian swords along with other weapons and suppose he were brave and a man of war and then a great and terrifying lion charged at him what do you think would the weapons turn its harm away without his drawing them and striking it is plain that they turn nothing away except by being wielded and by striking in just this way were a man to read and learn a hundred thousand points of knowledge and yet not act upon them they would profit him nothing except through action', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 17, NULL, 'Its likeness again is a man who has a fever and a bilious illness whose cure lies in certain medicines, yet the cure is not gained except by taking them. As the poet has said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'its likeness again is a man who has a fever and a bilious illness whose cure lies in certain medicines yet the cure is not gained except by taking them as the poet has said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 18, NULL, 'Verseكَرَمِي دُو هَزَارْ رَطْلْ هَمِي بِيْمَائِيتَا مَيْ نَخُورِي نَبَاشَدَتْ شَيْدَائِي"Even if you were to buy two thousand pounds of wine, it would not make you drunk until you drink it."', '', NULL, 'Verse', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'verseكرمي دو هزار رطل همي بيماييتا مي نخوري نباشدت شيدايي even if you were to buy two thousand pounds of wine it would not make you drunk until you drink it', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 19, NULL, 'And if you were to study knowledge for a hundred years and gather a thousand books, you would not be made ready for the mercy of Allah the Exalted except through action. Hear what the Quran says in this matter:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'and if you were to study knowledge for a hundred years and gather a thousand books you would not be made ready for the mercy of allah the exalted except through action hear what the quran says in this matter', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 20, NULL, 'An-Najm: 39وَأَن لَّيْسَ لِلْإِنسَنِ إِلَّا مَا سَعَىٰ"And that man shall have nothing but that for which he strives." (Quran, an-Najm: 38)', '', NULL, 'An-Najm: 39', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'an najm 39وان ليس للانسن الا ما سعي and that man shall have nothing but that for which he strives quran an najm 38', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 21, NULL, 'This establishes that a human being attains success only through genuine effort and striving. My dear beloved son, I know you may have read that this verse was abrogated. Let us grant that it was. But then what do you say about these words of Allah:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'this establishes that a human being attains success only through genuine effort and striving my dear beloved son i know you may have read that this verse was abrogated let us grant that it was but then what do you say about these words of allah', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 22, NULL, '"So whoever does an atom''s weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom''s weight of evil will see it." (Quran, 99:7 to 8)', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'so whoever does an atoms weight of good will see it and whoever does an atoms weight of evil will see it quran 99 7 to 8', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 23, NULL, 'Al-Kahf: 110فَمَنْ كَانَ يَرْجُو لِقَاءَ رَبِّهِ فَلْيَعْمَلْ عَمَلاً صَالِحًا"So whoever hopes for the meeting with his Lord, let him do righteous work." (Quran, al-Kahf: 110)', '', NULL, 'Al-Kahf: 110', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'al kahf 110فمن كان يرجو لقاء ربه فليعمل عملا صالحا so whoever hopes for the meeting with his lord let him do righteous work quran al kahf 110', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 24, NULL, 'Sayingإِنَّ الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحَاتِ كَانَتْ لَهُمْ جَنَّاتُ الْفِرْدَوْسِ نُزُلاً خَالِدِينَ فِيهَا لاَ يَبْغُونَ عَنْهَا حِوَلاً"Indeed, those who believe and do righteous deeds, for them the Gardens of Firdaws shall be a welcome abode, in which they will dwell forever, never desiring any removal from it."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'sayingان الذين امنوا وعملوا الصالحات كانت لهم جنات الفردوس نزلا خالدين فيها لا يبغون عنها حولا indeed those who believe and do righteous deeds for them the gardens of firdaws shall be a welcome abode in which they will dwell forever never desiring any removal from it', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 25, NULL, 'And in another place the Exalted has said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'and in another place the exalted has said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 26, NULL, 'Maryam: 60إِلَّا مَن تَابَ وَآمَنَ وَعَمِلَ صَلِحًۭا"Except for those who repented, and believed, and did righteous work."', '', NULL, 'Maryam: 60', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'maryam 60الا من تاب وامن وعمل صلحا except for those who repented and believed and did righteous work', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 27, NULL, 'And what do you say about these noble sayings of the Prophet ﷺ? He said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'and what do you say about these noble sayings of the prophet ﷺ he said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 28, NULL, '"Islam is built upon five things: to testify that there is none worthy of worship but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; to establish the prayer; to give the alms; to fast the month of Ramadan; and to perform the pilgrimage to the House for whoever is able to find a way to it."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'islam is built upon five things to testify that there is none worthy of worship but allah and that muhammad is the messenger of allah to establish the prayer to give the alms to fast the month of ramadan and to perform the pilgrimage to the house for whoever is able to find a way to it', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 29, NULL, 'And he said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'and he said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 30, NULL, '"Faith is affirmation with the tongue, assent with the heart, and action with the limbs."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'faith is affirmation with the tongue assent with the heart and action with the limbs', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 31, NULL, 'After all of this, should a thought arise in your heart that I am claiming a servant enters Paradise not by the grace of Allah but by his own deeds alone, then know that you have not grasped my meaning. On the contrary, what I am saying is that a servant enters Paradise through the favor, generosity, and blessings of Allah. Yet unless that servant makes himself deserving of Allah''s mercy through worship and obedience, he will not stand in a position to receive that mercy and favor. And I do not assert this on my own authority. Rather, Allah the Exalted Himself says:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'after all of this should a thought arise in your heart that i am claiming a servant enters paradise not by the grace of allah but by his own deeds alone then know that you have not grasped my meaning on the contrary what i am saying is that a servant enters paradise through the favor generosity and blessings of allah yet unless that servant makes himself deserving of allahs mercy through worship and obedience he will not stand in a position to receive that mercy and favor and i do not assert this on my own authority rather allah the exalted himself says', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 32, NULL, '"Indeed, the mercy of Allah is near to those who do good." (Al-Araf: 56)', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'indeed the mercy of allah is near to those who do good al araf 56', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'knowledge that will not save you', '1. Knowledge That Will Not Save You', 33, NULL, 'How, then, will a servant enter Paradise without the mercy of Allah the Exalted? I say this weighty point again: how will a servant enter Paradise without the mercy of Allah? And should someone argue that one enters Paradise on account of faith alone, then know that many formidable valleys lie ahead, that is, obstacles. The first and most daunting of them is to carry one''s faith safely to the very end.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 1 knowledge that will not save you', 'how then will a servant enter paradise without the mercy of allah the exalted i say this weighty point again how will a servant enter paradise without the mercy of allah and should someone argue that one enters paradise on account of faith alone then know that many formidable valleys lie ahead that is obstacles the first and most daunting of them is to carry ones faith safely to the very end', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 0, NULL, 'My dear son, hold fast to this truth: you will never find the reward without the effort that earns it.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'my dear son hold fast to this truth you will never find the reward without the effort that earns it', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 1, NULL, 'Let me tell you about a man among the Children of Israel who worshipped Allah the Exalted with great devotion for seventy years. Allah willed to display him to the angels, and so, to test him, He sent an angel down to him. The angel said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'let me tell you about a man among the children of israel who worshipped allah the exalted with great devotion for seventy years allah willed to display him to the angels and so to test him he sent an angel down to him the angel said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 2, NULL, '"Allah, the Holy, says that you have worn yourself out for nothing, for with all this worship you are still not fit to enter Paradise."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'allah the holy says that you have worn yourself out for nothing for with all this worship you are still not fit to enter paradise', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 3, NULL, 'When this message reached him, the worshipper answered:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'when this message reached him the worshipper answered', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 4, NULL, '"We were created to worship Him and to serve Him, and it is only right that we should serve Him, whatever comes of it. He owns all things and all people, myself among them, and He has power over all."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'we were created to worship him and to serve him and it is only right that we should serve him whatever comes of it he owns all things and all people myself among them and he has power over all', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 5, NULL, 'The angel then returned to the Presence of Allah the Exalted and said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'the angel then returned to the presence of allah the exalted and said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 6, NULL, '"O my Lord, You know best what Your servant has said."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'o my lord you know best what your servant has said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 7, NULL, 'Then Allah the Exalted said: since this servant of Ours does not turn his face away from Our worship and Our service, We too, in Our generosity, will not turn Our Face away from him.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'then allah the exalted said since this servant of ours does not turn his face away from our worship and our service we too in our generosity will not turn our face away from him', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 8, NULL, 'Sayingاِشْهَدُوا يَا مَلَائِكَتِي أَنِّي قَدْ غَفَرْتُ لَهُ"O My angels, bear witness, all of you, that I have forgiven him."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'sayingاشهدوا يا ملايكتي اني قد غفرت له o my angels bear witness all of you that i have forgiven him', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 9, NULL, 'My dear son, so long as you do not act, you will not find the reward. Hear now what the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) says:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'my dear son so long as you do not act you will not find the reward hear now what the messenger of allah may allah bless him and grant him peace says', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 10, NULL, 'Prophetic traditionحَاسِبُوا أَنْفُسَكُمْ قَبْلَ أَنْ تُحَاسَبُوا، وَ زِنُوا أَعْمَالَكُمْ قَبْلَ أَنْ تُوزَنُوا"Take account of yourselves before you are taken to account, and weigh your deeds — that is, examine them and correct their shortcomings — before your deeds are weighed and judged."', '', NULL, 'Prophetic tradition', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'prophetic traditionحاسبوا انفسكم قبل ان تحاسبوا و زنوا اعمالكم قبل ان توزنوا take account of yourselves before you are taken to account and weigh your deeds that is examine them and correct their shortcomings before your deeds are weighed and judged', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 11, NULL, 'Ali (رض) said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'ali رض said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 12, NULL, 'Sayingمَنْ ظَنَّ أَنَّهُ بِدُونِ الْجَهْدِ يَصِلُ فَهُوَ مُتَمَنٍّ. وَمَنْ ظَنَّ أَنَّهُ بِبَذْلِ الْجُهْدِ يَصِلُ فَهُوَ مُسْتَغْنٍ"Whoever supposes that he will arrive without striving is a wishful dreamer, and whoever supposes that he will arrive by his own effort alone presumes himself in no need."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'sayingمن ظن انه بدون الجهد يصل فهو متمن ومن ظن انه ببذل الجهد يصل فهو مستغن whoever supposes that he will arrive without striving is a wishful dreamer and whoever supposes that he will arrive by his own effort alone presumes himself in no need', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 13, NULL, 'Hasan al-Basri said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'hasan al basri said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 14, NULL, 'SayingHasan al-Basriطَلَبُ الْجَنَّةِ بِلَا عَمَلٍ ذَنْبٌ مِنَ الذُّنُوبِ"To seek Paradise without deeds is a sin among the sins."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'sayinghasan al basriطلب الجنه بلا عمل ذنب من الذنوب to seek paradise without deeds is a sin among the sins', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 15, NULL, 'And another intimate of Allah said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'and another intimate of allah said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 16, NULL, 'Sayingعَلَامَةُ الْحَقِيقَةِ تَرْكُ مُلَاحَظَةِ الْعَمَلِ لَا تَرْكُ الْعَمَلِ"The mark of realized truth is to leave off admiring one''s own action, not to leave off the action itself."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'sayingعلامه الحقيقه ترك ملاحظه العمل لا ترك العمل the mark of realized truth is to leave off admiring ones own action not to leave off the action itself', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 17, NULL, 'One acts without regarding one''s own act with pride, yet one must never abandon the doing of good deeds, even where the danger of self-admiration is present.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'one acts without regarding ones own act with pride yet one must never abandon the doing of good deeds even where the danger of self admiration is present', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 18, NULL, 'Our master of both worlds, the Prophet Muhammad, expresses this truth more eloquently, more clearly, and more beautifully than any of the sayings above:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'our master of both worlds the prophet muhammad expresses this truth more eloquently more clearly and more beautifully than any of the sayings above', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 19, NULL, 'Sayingالْكَيِّسُ مَنْ دَانَ نَفْسَهُ وَ عَمِلَ لِمَا بَعْدَ الْمَوْتِ، وَ الْأَحْمَقُ مَنِ اتَّبَعَ هَوَاهُ وَتَمَنَّى عَلَى اللَّهِ تَعَالَى الْأَمَانِيَّ"The intelligent one is he who calls his own soul to account and works for what lies after death; and the fool is he who follows the desires of his soul and yet indulges vain hopes upon Allah the Exalted."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'sayingالكيس من دان نفسه و عمل لما بعد الموت و الاحمق من اتبع هواه وتمني علي الله تعالي الاماني the intelligent one is he who calls his own soul to account and works for what lies after death and the fool is he who follows the desires of his soul and yet indulges vain hopes upon allah the exalted', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 20, NULL, 'Some narrations add the words: and hopes for Allah''s forgiveness.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'some narrations add the words and hopes for allahs forgiveness', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 21, NULL, 'My dear son, how many nights have you stayed awake, going over your knowledge and poring over books, denying yourself sleep? I do not know what your motive was. If it was to grasp some share of this world, to draw in its worthless goods, to secure its offices, and to boast over your peers and equals, then:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'my dear son how many nights have you stayed awake going over your knowledge and poring over books denying yourself sleep i do not know what your motive was if it was to grasp some share of this world to draw in its worthless goods to secure its offices and to boast over your peers and equals then', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 22, NULL, 'Sayingفَوَيْلٌ لَكَ ثُمَّ وَيْلٌ لَكَ"Woe unto you, and again woe unto you."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'sayingفويل لك ثم ويل لك woe unto you and again woe unto you', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 23, NULL, 'But if your aim in it was to revive the way of the Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace), to refine your character, and to break the soul that commands to evil, then:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'but if your aim in it was to revive the way of the prophet may allah bless him and grant him peace to refine your character and to break the soul that commands to evil then', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 24, NULL, 'Sayingفَطُوبَى لَكَ ثُمَّ طُوبَى لَكَ"Blessedness be yours, and again blessedness be yours."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'sayingفطوبي لك ثم طوبي لك blessedness be yours and again blessedness be yours', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 25, NULL, 'How well he spoke who said in verse:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'how well he spoke who said in verse', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 26, NULL, 'Verseسَهَرُ الْعُيُونِ لِغَيْرِ وَجْهِكَ ضَائِعٌوَ بُكَاؤُهُنَّ لِغَيْرِ فَقْدِكَ بَاطِلٌ"The wakefulness of the eyes for anything but Your Countenance is wasted, and their weeping for the loss of anything but You is in vain."', '', NULL, 'Verse', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'verseسهر العيون لغير وجهك ضايعو بكاوهن لغير فقدك باطل the wakefulness of the eyes for anything but your countenance is wasted and their weeping for the loss of anything but you is in vain', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 27, NULL, 'My dear son, hear this prophetic tradition:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'my dear son hear this prophetic tradition', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 28, NULL, 'Prophetic traditionعِشْ مَا شِئْتَ فَإِنَّكَ مَيِّتٌ، وَ أَحْبِبْ مَا شِئْتَ فَإِنَّكَ مُفَارِقُهُ؛ وَ اِعْمَلْ مَا شِئْتَ فَإِنَّكَ مَجْزِيٌّ بِهِ"Live as you wish, for you shall surely die. Love what you wish, for you shall surely be parted from it. And do what you wish, for you shall surely be recompensed for it."', '', NULL, 'Prophetic tradition', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'prophetic traditionعش ما شيت فانك ميت و احبب ما شيت فانك مفارقه و اعمل ما شيت فانك مجزي به live as you wish for you shall surely die love what you wish for you shall surely be parted from it and do what you wish for you shall surely be recompensed for it', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the striving that mercy meets', '2. The Striving That Mercy Meets', 29, NULL, 'Editorial note (tradition-grounded). A related teaching preserved in this same tradition warns the reader plainly: do not neglect good deeds, and be firmly convinced that knowledge alone, without righteous action, will not benefit you on the Day of Judgment — which is exactly the conviction the story of the seventy-year worshipper dramatizes here. The wider tradition also records the assurance that whoever says there is no god but Allah will enter Paradise, but with a qualification worth holding beside this chapter''s counsel: that it be said with sincerity. Read together, the two cautions frame the same narrow path the chapter walks — deeds are indispensable and reward comes only through striving, yet the striving must not curdle into self-regard. One acts fully, and leaves off admiring the act.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 2 the striving that mercy meets', 'editorial note tradition grounded a related teaching preserved in this same tradition warns the reader plainly do not neglect good deeds and be firmly convinced that knowledge alone without righteous action will not benefit you on the day of judgment which is exactly the conviction the story of the seventy year worshipper dramatizes here the wider tradition also records the assurance that whoever says there is no god but allah will enter paradise but with a qualification worth holding beside this chapters counsel that it be said with sincerity read together the two cautions frame the same narrow path the chapter walks deeds are indispensable and reward comes only through striving yet the striving must not curdle into self regard one acts fully and leaves off admiring the act', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 0, NULL, 'My dear son, after all these years of study, what have you truly gained for yourself? Look at the sciences you have chased after: theology (عِلْمُ الْكَلاَم), the science of juristic disagreement (الْخِلَاف), medicine (الطبّ), the registers and chronicles (اَلدَّوَاوِين), poetry (الْأَشْعَار), astronomy (نَجْم), prosody (اَلْعَرُوض), grammar (النّحو), and morphology (التَّصْرِيف). What has any of it given you, beyond the waste of your own life and a small measure of fame among people? I have read in the Gospel (إِنْجِيلْ) of the Prophet Isa (ع) that from the moment a body is laid on the bier and carried out, until it is set down at the edge of the grave, Allah in His majesty puts forty questions to it. The first is this:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'my dear son after all these years of study what have you truly gained for yourself look at the sciences you have chased after theology علم الكلام the science of juristic disagreement الخلاف medicine الطب the registers and chronicles الدواوين poetry الاشعار astronomy نجم prosody العروض grammar النحو and morphology التصريف what has any of it given you beyond the waste of your own life and a small measure of fame among people i have read in the gospel انجيل of the prophet isa ع that from the moment a body is laid on the bier and carried out until it is set down at the edge of the grave allah in his majesty puts forty questions to it the first is this', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 1, NULL, 'Sayingعَبْدِي طَهَّرْتَ مَنْظَرَ الْخَلْقِ سِنِينَ وَ مَا طَهَّرْتَ مَنْظَرِي سَاعَةً"My servant, for years you cleansed and adorned your outward self for the eyes of My creation, yet you did not cleanse the place upon which I gaze, your heart, for even a single hour."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'sayingعبدي طهرت منظر الخلق سنين و ما طهرت منظري ساعه my servant for years you cleansed and adorned your outward self for the eyes of my creation yet you did not cleanse the place upon which i gaze your heart for even a single hour', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 2, NULL, 'My dear son, every day He looks into your heart and says:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'my dear son every day he looks into your heart and says', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 3, NULL, 'Sayingكُلُّ يَوْمٍ يَنْظُرُ فِي قَلْبِكَ يَقُولُ: مَا تَصْنَعُ لِغَيْرِي وَأَنْتَ مَحْفُوفٌ بِخَيْرِي"What are you doing for the sake of another, when My bounty surrounds you on every side? Yet you are deaf, and you do not hear."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'sayingكل يوم ينظر في قلبك يقول ما تصنع لغيري وانت محفوف بخيري what are you doing for the sake of another when my bounty surrounds you on every side yet you are deaf and you do not hear', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 4, NULL, 'My dear son, knowledge without action is madness, and action without knowledge cannot stand. Know this well: the knowledge that does not keep you far from disobedience today, and does not carry you toward obedience, will not keep you far from the Fire of Hell tomorrow. If you do not act on your knowledge today, and do not make good the days that have already slipped away from you, then on the Day of Resurrection you will cry out:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'my dear son knowledge without action is madness and action without knowledge cannot stand know this well the knowledge that does not keep you far from disobedience today and does not carry you toward obedience will not keep you far from the fire of hell tomorrow if you do not act on your knowledge today and do not make good the days that have already slipped away from you then on the day of resurrection you will cry out', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 5, NULL, 'As-Sajdah: 12فَٱرْجِعْنَا نَعْمَلْ صَلِحًا"Send us back, that we may do righteous deeds!"', '', NULL, 'As-Sajdah: 12', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'as sajdah 12فارجعنا نعمل صلحا send us back that we may do righteous deeds', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 6, NULL, 'And you will be answered:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'and you will be answered', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 7, NULL, 'Sayingيَا أَحْمَقُ أَنْتَ مِنْ هُنَاكَ تَجِيءُ"O fool, it is from there that you have just come."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'sayingيا احمق انت من هناك تجيء o fool it is from there that you have just come', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 8, NULL, 'My dear son, fix your resolve upon the spirit, defeat upon the lower self, and death before the body, for your dwelling place is the grave, and the people of the graves await you at every moment, watching for the hour you will reach them. Beware, and beware again, of coming to them empty-handed, with no provision. Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (رض) said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'my dear son fix your resolve upon the spirit defeat upon the lower self and death before the body for your dwelling place is the grave and the people of the graves await you at every moment watching for the hour you will reach them beware and beware again of coming to them empty handed with no provision abu bakr al siddiq رض said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 9, NULL, 'Sayingهَٰذِهِ الْأَجْسَادُ قَفَصُ الطُّيُورِ أَوْ إِصْطَبْلُ الدَّوَابِّ"These bodies are either cages for birds or stables for beasts."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'sayingهذه الاجساد قفص الطيور او اصطبل الدواب these bodies are either cages for birds or stables for beasts', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 10, NULL, 'Reflect, then, upon yourself: which of the two are you? If you are among the birds of the higher world, then when you hear the call sounded, «ٱرْجِعِىٓ إِلَىٰ رَبِّكِ», "Return to your Lord," you will take flight upward until you settle in the highest towers of the Gardens. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'reflect then upon yourself which of the two are you if you are among the birds of the higher world then when you hear the call sounded ارجعي الي ربك return to your lord you will take flight upward until you settle in the highest towers of the gardens so the messenger of allah ﷺ said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 11, NULL, 'Sayingاِهْتَزَّ عَرْشُ الرَّحْمَنِ مِنْ مَوْتِ سَعْدٍ بْنِ مُعَاذٍ"The Throne of the Most Merciful trembled at the death of Sa''d ibn Muadh."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'sayingاهتز عرش الرحمن من موت سعد بن معاذ the throne of the most merciful trembled at the death of sad ibn muadh', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 12, NULL, 'But Allah preserve you from being among the beasts, of whom Allah the Exalted has said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'but allah preserve you from being among the beasts of whom allah the exalted has said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 13, NULL, 'Al-A''raf: 179أُولَئِكَ كَالْأَنْعَامِ بَلْ هُمْ أَضَلُّ"Those are like cattle; rather, they are further astray." (al-Araf: 179)', '', NULL, 'Al-A''raf: 179', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'al araf 179اوليك كالانعام بل هم اضل those are like cattle rather they are further astray al araf 179', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 14, NULL, 'If that is your state, then do not feel safe from being carried from the زَاوِيَة, the corner of your dwelling, to the hawiya, the pit of the Fire, passing straight from this world into the flames. It is related that Hasan al-Basri (may Allah have mercy on him) was once handed a drink of cold water. He took the cup in his hand, and a faintness came over him so that it fell from his grasp. When he came to himself, he was asked, "What is the matter with you, O Abu Said?" He said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'if that is your state then do not feel safe from being carried from the زاويه the corner of your dwelling to the hawiya the pit of the fire passing straight from this world into the flames it is related that hasan al basri may allah have mercy on him was once handed a drink of cold water he took the cup in his hand and a faintness came over him so that it fell from his grasp when he came to himself he was asked what is the matter with you o abu said he said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 15, NULL, 'Sayingذُكِرَتْ أُمْنِيَّةُ أَهْلِ النَّارِ حِينَ يَقُولُونَ لِأَهْلِ الْجَنَّةِ: أَنْ أَفِيضُوا عَلَيْنَا مِنَ الْمَاءِ أَوْ مِمَّا رَزَقَكُمُ اللهُ"I remembered the longing of the people of the Fire, when they will call out to the people of the Garden: Pour down upon us some water, or some of that which Allah has provided you." (al-Araf: 50)', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'sayingذكرت امنيه اهل النار حين يقولون لاهل الجنه ان افيضوا علينا من الماء او مما رزقكم الله i remembered the longing of the people of the fire when they will call out to the people of the garden pour down upon us some water or some of that which allah has provided you al araf 50', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 16, NULL, 'My dear son, if bare knowledge were enough for you, and you needed no deed beside it, then the call, «هَلْ مِنْ سَائِلٍ؟ هَلْ مِنْ مُسْتَغْفِرٍ؟ هَلْ مِنْ تَائِبٍ؟», "Is there any who asks? Is there any who seeks forgiveness? Is there any who repents?", would be a thing sent out in vain, with no purpose. But it is not so.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'my dear son if bare knowledge were enough for you and you needed no deed beside it then the call هل من سايل هل من مستغفر هل من تايب is there any who asks is there any who seeks forgiveness is there any who repents would be a thing sent out in vain with no purpose but it is not so', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 17, NULL, 'Once a group of Companions were praising Abdullah ibn Umar (رض) in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'once a group of companions were praising abdullah ibn umar رض in the presence of the messenger of allah ﷺ and he said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 18, NULL, 'Sayingنِعْمَ الرَّجُلُ هُوَ لَوْ كَانَ يُصَلِّي بِاللَّيْلِ"He is an excellent man, if only he would pray in the night."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'sayingنعم الرجل هو لو كان يصلي بالليل he is an excellent man if only he would pray in the night', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 19, NULL, 'And on another occasion he said to one of his Companions:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'and on another occasion he said to one of his companions', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 20, NULL, 'Sayingلَا تُكْثِرِ النَّوْمَ بِاللَّيْلِ فَإِنَّ كَثْرَةَ النَّوْمِ بِاللَّيْلِ يَدَعُ صَاحِبَهُ فَقِيرًا يَوْمَ الْقِيَامَةِ"Do not sleep much in the night, for much sleep in the night leaves its owner destitute on the Day of Resurrection."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'sayingلا تكثر النوم بالليل فان كثره النوم بالليل يدع صاحبه فقيرا يوم القيامه do not sleep much in the night for much sleep in the night leaves its owner destitute on the day of resurrection', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 21, NULL, 'My dear son, weigh these three verses and what is placed within each of them.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'my dear son weigh these three verses and what is placed within each of them', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 22, NULL, 'The first:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'the first', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 23, NULL, 'Sayingوَمِنَ اللَّيْلِ فَتَهَجَّدْ بِهِ نَافِلَةً لَكَ"And in part of the night, keep vigil in prayer with it, as a voluntary act for you." (al-Isra: 79)', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'sayingومن الليل فتهجد به نافله لك and in part of the night keep vigil in prayer with it as a voluntary act for you al isra 79', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 24, NULL, 'In this there is a command.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'in this there is a command', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 25, NULL, 'The second:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'the second', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 26, NULL, 'Adh-Dhariyat: 18وَبِالْأَسْحَارِ هُمْ يَسْتَغْفِرُونَ"And in the hours before dawn they would seek forgiveness." (al-Dhariyat: 18)', '', NULL, 'Adh-Dhariyat: 18', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'adh dhariyat 18وبالاسحار هم يستغفرون and in the hours before dawn they would seek forgiveness al dhariyat 18', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 27, NULL, 'In this there is the giving of thanks.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'in this there is the giving of thanks', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 28, NULL, 'The third:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'the third', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 29, NULL, 'Ali ''Imran: 17وَالْمُسْتَغْفِرِينَ بِالْأَسْحَارِ"And those who seek forgiveness in the hours before dawn." (Al Imran: 17)', '', NULL, 'Ali ''Imran: 17', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'ali imran 17والمستغفرين بالاسحار and those who seek forgiveness in the hours before dawn al imran 17', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 30, NULL, 'In this there is honorable mention.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'in this there is honorable mention', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 31, NULL, 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'the messenger of allah ﷺ said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 32, NULL, 'Prophetic traditionثَلَاثَةُ أَصْوَاتٍ يُحِبُّهَا اللَّهُ تَعَالَى: صَوْتُ الدِّيكِ، وَصَوْتُ الَّذِي يَقْرَأُ الْقُرْآنَ، وَصَوْتُ الْمُسْتَغْفِرِينَ بِالْأَسْحَارِ"There are three sounds Allah the Exalted loves: the voice of the rooster, the voice of the one who recites the Quran, and the voice of those who seek forgiveness in the hours before dawn."', '', NULL, 'Prophetic tradition', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'prophetic traditionثلاثه اصوات يحبها الله تعالي صوت الديك وصوت الذي يقرا القران وصوت المستغفرين بالاسحار there are three sounds allah the exalted loves the voice of the rooster the voice of the one who recites the quran and the voice of those who seek forgiveness in the hours before dawn', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 33, NULL, 'Sufyan al-Thawri (may Allah have mercy on him) said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'sufyan al thawri may allah have mercy on him said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 34, NULL, 'SayingSufyan al-Thawriإِنَّ اللَّهَ تَبَارَكَ وَتَعَالَىٰ خَلَقَ رِيحًا تَهُبُّ بِالْأَسْحَارِ تَحْمِلُ الْأَذْكَارَ وَالِاسْتِغْفَارَ إِلَىٰ الْمَلِكِ الْجَبَّارِ"Allah, blessed and exalted, created a wind that blows in the hours before dawn, carrying the words of remembrance and the seeking of forgiveness up to the Sovereign, the Compeller."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'sayingsufyan al thawriان الله تبارك وتعالي خلق ريحا تهب بالاسحار تحمل الاذكار والاستغفار الي الملك الجبار allah blessed and exalted created a wind that blows in the hours before dawn carrying the words of remembrance and the seeking of forgiveness up to the sovereign the compeller', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 35, NULL, 'And he also said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'and he also said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 36, NULL, 'Sayingإِذَا كَانَ أَوَّلُ اللَّيْلِ نَادَىٰ مُنَادٍ مِنْ تَحْتِ الْعَرْشِ: أَلَا لِيَقُمِ الْعَابِدُونَ. فَيَقُومُونَ وَيُصَلُّونَ مَا شَاءَ اللَّهُ. ثُمَّ يُنَادِي مُنَادٍ فِي شَطْرِ اللَّيْلِ: أَلَا لِيَقُمِ الْقَانِتُونَ. فَيَقُومُونَ وَيُصَلُّونَ إِلَى السَّحَرِ. فَإِذَا كَانَ السَّحَرُ نَادَى مُنَادٍ: أَلَا لِيَقُمِ الْمُسْتَغْفِرُونَ. فَيَقُومُونَ وَيَسْتَغْفِرُونَ. فَإِذَا طَلَعَ الْفَجْرُ نَادَى مُنَادٍ: أَلَا لِيَقُمِ الْغَافِلُونَ. فَيَقُومُونَ مِنْ فُرُشِهِمْ كَالْمَوْتَى نُشِرُوا مِنْ قُبُورِهِمْ"When the first part of the night comes, a caller cries out from beneath the Throne: Let the worshippers arise. So they arise and pray as long as Allah wills. Then, at the middle of the night, a caller cries out: Let the devout arise. So they arise and pray until the hour before dawn. When that hour arrives, a caller cries out: Let those who seek forgiveness arise. So they arise and seek forgiveness. And when dawn breaks, a caller cries out: Let the heedless arise. So they rise from their beds like the dead raised up from their graves."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'sayingاذا كان اول الليل نادي مناد من تحت العرش الا ليقم العابدون فيقومون ويصلون ما شاء الله ثم ينادي مناد في شطر الليل الا ليقم القانتون فيقومون ويصلون الي السحر فاذا كان السحر نادي مناد الا ليقم المستغفرون فيقومون ويستغفرون فاذا طلع الفجر نادي مناد الا ليقم الغافلون فيقومون من فرشهم كالموتي نشروا من قبورهم when the first part of the night comes a caller cries out from beneath the throne let the worshippers arise so they arise and pray as long as allah wills then at the middle of the night a caller cries out let the devout arise so they arise and pray until the hour before dawn when that hour arrives a caller cries out let those who seek forgiveness arise so they arise and seek forgiveness and when dawn breaks a caller cries out let the heedless arise so they rise from their beds like the dead raised up from their graves', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 37, NULL, 'My dear son, it is related in the counsels of Luqman the Wise (ع) to his son that he said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'my dear son it is related in the counsels of luqman the wise ع to his son that he said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 38, NULL, 'Sayingيَا بُنَيَّ، لَا يَكُونَنَّ الدِّيكُ أَكْيَسَ مِنْكَ! يُنَادِي بِالْأَسْحَارِ وَأَنْتَ نَائِمٌ"My son, do not let the rooster prove shrewder than you, calling out in the hours before dawn while you lie asleep."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'sayingيا بني لا يكونن الديك اكيس منك ينادي بالاسحار وانت نايم my son do not let the rooster prove shrewder than you calling out in the hours before dawn while you lie asleep', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 39, NULL, 'How well the poet spoke who said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'how well the poet spoke who said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'the hours before dawn', '3. The Hours Before Dawn', 40, NULL, 'Verseلَقَدْ هَتَفَتْ فِي جُنْحِ لَيْلٍ حَمَامَةٌعلى فننٍ وهناً، وإنّي لنائمكذبتُ، وبيتِ الله، لو كنتُ عاشقاًلَمَّا سَبَقَتْنِي بِالْبُكَاءِ الْحَمَائِمُوأزعم أنّي هائمٌ ذو صبابةٍلِرَبِّي، فَلَا أَبْكِي وَتَبْكِي الْبَهَائِمُ"In the dark of night a dove cried out upon a swaying branch, while I lay fast asleep. I have lied, by the House of Allah! Had I truly been a lover, the doves would never have outstripped me in weeping. I claim to be one distracted, consumed with yearning for my Lord, yet I do not weep, while the very beasts weep on."', '', NULL, 'Verse', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 3 the hours before dawn', 'verseلقد هتفت في جنح ليل حمامهعلي فنن وهنا واني لنايمكذبت وبيت الله لو كنت عاشقالما سبقتني بالبكاء الحمايموازعم اني هايم ذو صبابهلربي فلا ابكي وتبكي البهايم in the dark of night a dove cried out upon a swaying branch while i lay fast asleep i have lied by the house of allah had i truly been a lover the doves would never have outstripped me in weeping i claim to be one distracted consumed with yearning for my lord yet i do not weep while the very beasts weep on', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 0, NULL, 'My dear beloved son! What I want here is for you to understand truly what obedience and worship are. Obedience and worship mean following the Shariah, the Divine Law of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ — whether its commandments call for doing righteous deeds or for holding back from evil, and whether they concern his words or his example. In short, whatever you do or do not do, whatever you say or leave unsaid, all of it must accord fully with his teaching. If you speak, speak according to his teaching. If you keep silent, keep silent according to his guidance. If you act or refrain from acting, do so in keeping with his example.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'my dear beloved son what i want here is for you to understand truly what obedience and worship are obedience and worship mean following the shariah the divine law of the prophet muhammad ﷺ whether its commandments call for doing righteous deeds or for holding back from evil and whether they concern his words or his example in short whatever you do or do not do whatever you say or leave unsaid all of it must accord fully with his teaching if you speak speak according to his teaching if you keep silent keep silent according to his guidance if you act or refrain from acting do so in keeping with his example', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 1, NULL, 'Understand this well: if you do something believing it to be worship, yet you do not do it in obedience to his command, it will not count as worship at all. It will count as a sin, even when it wears the outward form of prayer or fasting. Do you not know that a person who fasts on either of the two days of Eid, or on the days of Hajj, commits a sin? It has the outward shape of fasting, but it is not done in accordance with the command, and so the one who does it is a wrongdoer. In the same way, whoever prays at a forbidden time — such as after the عَصْر prayer before sunset, or after the فَجْر prayer before the sun has risen far enough — or prays upon unlawfully occupied ground, is called أَثِيْم, a sinner, and فَاسِقْ, a transgressor. Though the act resembles worship, it is not sound, because it was not done according to the guidance of the Prophet ﷺ. Yet when a husband and wife come together in lawful intimacy, this is no sin, however it may look to the uninformed, for it falls within his guidance and is therefore halal, permissible. So it is established that worship is only another name for obedience. Worship means obedience.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'understand this well if you do something believing it to be worship yet you do not do it in obedience to his command it will not count as worship at all it will count as a sin even when it wears the outward form of prayer or fasting do you not know that a person who fasts on either of the two days of eid or on the days of hajj commits a sin it has the outward shape of fasting but it is not done in accordance with the command and so the one who does it is a wrongdoer in the same way whoever prays at a forbidden time such as after the عصر prayer before sunset or after the فجر prayer before the sun has risen far enough or prays upon unlawfully occupied ground is called اثيم a sinner and فاسق a transgressor though the act resembles worship it is not sound because it was not done according to the guidance of the prophet ﷺ yet when a husband and wife come together in lawful intimacy this is no sin however it may look to the uninformed for it falls within his guidance and is therefore halal permissible so it is established that worship is only another name for obedience worship means obedience', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 2, NULL, 'In the very same way, prayer and fasting count as worship only when they are offered according to the Divine guidelines. Therefore, my dear beloved son, let all your words and all your deeds conform to the commandments of the Prophet ﷺ. Whatever you do and whatever you say must accord with the Shariah, for any knowledge or action of God''s creation that falls outside the Shariah is plain misguidance, and it draws a human being away from the truth. This is why the Prophet ﷺ abrogated all the religious dispensations that came before him.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'in the very same way prayer and fasting count as worship only when they are offered according to the divine guidelines therefore my dear beloved son let all your words and all your deeds conform to the commandments of the prophet ﷺ whatever you do and whatever you say must accord with the shariah for any knowledge or action of gods creation that falls outside the shariah is plain misguidance and it draws a human being away from the truth this is why the prophet ﷺ abrogated all the religious dispensations that came before him', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 3, NULL, 'So do nothing without his guidance, and be certain of this: you cannot advance upon the Path of Allah the Exalted through accumulated knowledge alone. This Path is traveled by earnest effort and مُجَاهَدَة, the intense inner struggle and disciplined exercise of the soul. The pleasures and demands of the lower self must be cut away with the sword of مُجَاهَدَة. These desires cannot be uprooted through immorality, nor through the deceptions of false Sufis — those who claim the station of Sufism and imitate its outward language and appearance while remaining hollow and devious within. The true Sufis are those who have attained an intimate nearness to God through the emergence of purified souls, having driven evil out of the deepest recesses of their being by genuine مُجَاهَدَة. Allah the Exalted takes no delight in subtleties of philosophy spun amid a life engulfed in sin and darkness. A tongue that utters words of profound wisdom and beauty while the heart stays enslaved to heedlessness and the appetites of the lower self is a mark of the gravest misfortune.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'so do nothing without his guidance and be certain of this you cannot advance upon the path of allah the exalted through accumulated knowledge alone this path is traveled by earnest effort and مجاهده the intense inner struggle and disciplined exercise of the soul the pleasures and demands of the lower self must be cut away with the sword of مجاهده these desires cannot be uprooted through immorality nor through the deceptions of false sufis those who claim the station of sufism and imitate its outward language and appearance while remaining hollow and devious within the true sufis are those who have attained an intimate nearness to god through the emergence of purified souls having driven evil out of the deepest recesses of their being by genuine مجاهده allah the exalted takes no delight in subtleties of philosophy spun amid a life engulfed in sin and darkness a tongue that utters words of profound wisdom and beauty while the heart stays enslaved to heedlessness and the appetites of the lower self is a mark of the gravest misfortune', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 4, NULL, 'Unless you cut away the desires and inclinations of the lower self with the sword of truth and مُجَاهَدَة, the light of الْمَعْرِفَة — that deep and intimate knowledge and conscious relationship with Allah — will never dawn within your heart.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'unless you cut away the desires and inclinations of the lower self with the sword of truth and مجاهده the light of المعرفه that deep and intimate knowledge and conscious relationship with allah will never dawn within your heart', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 5, NULL, 'My dear beloved son! You have asked me about certain spiritual matters, and some of them cannot be fully set down in writing or in speech. When you reach that high station of enlightenment, you will come to know them of yourself. The lesson of love is not taught; it is born and learned from within. If you reach that state, knowing these things will be like passing from one spiritual station into another. For عِشْق, intense love, is another name for love and for the fervent longing to seek the Beloved, which is ذَوْق. Love and ذَوْق can be conveyed neither in speech nor in writing, and their true essence cannot be laid before another. Just as sweetness and sourness and bitterness cannot be adequately described by anyone who tries to put them into words, so it is here. Know, then, that if you arrive at this destination of high station, you will come to know all of this by yourself; but if you cannot reach it, the truth can be described to you only so far as writing and speech allow.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'my dear beloved son you have asked me about certain spiritual matters and some of them cannot be fully set down in writing or in speech when you reach that high station of enlightenment you will come to know them of yourself the lesson of love is not taught it is born and learned from within if you reach that state knowing these things will be like passing from one spiritual station into another for عشق intense love is another name for love and for the fervent longing to seek the beloved which is ذوق love and ذوق can be conveyed neither in speech nor in writing and their true essence cannot be laid before another just as sweetness and sourness and bitterness cannot be adequately described by anyone who tries to put them into words so it is here know then that if you arrive at this destination of high station you will come to know all of this by yourself but if you cannot reach it the truth can be described to you only so far as writing and speech allow', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 6, NULL, 'My dear beloved son! Some of your questions are of this first kind. As for those that can be answered in writing and speech, I have already addressed them in detail in my book Ahya al-Uloom ad-Deen, the Revival of the Knowledge of the Path to God, and in other works, which you may read. Even so, I will explain them here in a concise manner, God willing.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'my dear beloved son some of your questions are of this first kind as for those that can be answered in writing and speech i have already addressed them in detail in my book ahya al uloom ad deen the revival of the knowledge of the path to god and in other works which you may read even so i will explain them here in a concise manner god willing', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 7, NULL, 'Secondly, you have asked what is required of those who walk upon the Path of Allah the Exalted, the sincere seekers of Him. Four things are obligatory upon such a seeker.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'secondly you have asked what is required of those who walk upon the path of allah the exalted the sincere seekers of him four things are obligatory upon such a seeker', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 8, NULL, 'The first condition is that you hold a conviction and fear of Allah so firm that you never fall into innovation, بِدْعَة, which is any matter contrary to the established principles of the Quran and the Sunnah. The Sunnah encompasses the teachings, actions, outward conduct, and inner states of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ — such as contentment, patience, gratitude, certitude in God, intimacy with Him, الْمَعْرِفَة, the deep and intimate knowledge and recognition of God, intense love of God, and the like.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'the first condition is that you hold a conviction and fear of allah so firm that you never fall into innovation بدعه which is any matter contrary to the established principles of the quran and the sunnah the sunnah encompasses the teachings actions outward conduct and inner states of the prophet muhammad ﷺ such as contentment patience gratitude certitude in god intimacy with him المعرفه the deep and intimate knowledge and recognition of god intense love of god and the like', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 9, NULL, 'The second condition is that you make a true and complete repentance, تَوْبَةٌ نَصُوح, of such a kind that you will never return to the humiliation of sins.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'the second condition is that you make a true and complete repentance توبه نصوح of such a kind that you will never return to the humiliation of sins', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 10, NULL, 'The third condition is that you keep even your enemies satisfied, so that none of God''s creation holds any unfulfilled claim against you.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'the third condition is that you keep even your enemies satisfied so that none of gods creation holds any unfulfilled claim against you', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 11, NULL, 'The fourth condition is that you acquire enough knowledge of the Shariah to be able to obey the commandments of Allah the Exalted that are obligatory upon you. You are not required to learn more of the Shariah than that, and of the other branches of knowledge you should learn only so much as is obligatory.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'the fourth condition is that you acquire enough knowledge of the shariah to be able to obey the commandments of allah the exalted that are obligatory upon you you are not required to learn more of the shariah than that and of the other branches of knowledge you should learn only so much as is obligatory', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 12, NULL, 'Know that it has been narrated in the traditions of the intimate friends of Allah, the saints, that Shibli (رض) said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'know that it has been narrated in the traditions of the intimate friends of allah the saints that shibli رض said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 13, NULL, '"I served four hundred spiritual masters and guides, and out of the four thousand traditions of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ that they narrated to me, I adopted and acted upon only one, and I left the rest aside. For when I reflected upon this single hadith, I found my salvation within it. I found in this one hadith the knowledge of all who came first and all who came last:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'i served four hundred spiritual masters and guides and out of the four thousand traditions of the prophet muhammad ﷺ that they narrated to me i adopted and acted upon only one and i left the rest aside for when i reflected upon this single hadith i found my salvation within it i found in this one hadith the knowledge of all who came first and all who came last', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 14, NULL, 'Sayingإِعْمَلْ لِدُنْيَاكَ بِقَدْرِ مَقَامِكَ فِيهَا، وَاعْمَلْ لِآخِرَتِكَ بِقَدْرِ بَقَائِكَ فِيهَا، وَاعْمَلْ لِلَّهِ بِقَدْرِ حَاجَتِكَ إِلَيْهِ، وَاعْمَلْ لِلنَّارِ بِقَدْرِ صَبْرِكَ عَلَيْهَا', '', 'إِعْمَلْ لِدُنْيَاكَ بِقَدْرِ مَقَامِكَ فِيهَا، وَاعْمَلْ لِآخِرَتِكَ بِقَدْرِ بَقَائِكَ فِيهَا، وَاعْمَلْ لِلَّهِ بِقَدْرِ حَاجَتِكَ إِلَيْهِ، وَاعْمَلْ لِلنَّارِ بِقَدْرِ صَبْرِكَ عَلَيْهَا', 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'sayingاعمل لدنياك بقدر مقامك فيها واعمل لاخرتك بقدر بقايك فيها واعمل لله بقدر حاجتك اليه واعمل للنار بقدر صبرك عليها', 'اعمل لدنياك بقدر مقامك فيها واعمل لاخرتك بقدر بقايك فيها واعمل لله بقدر حاجتك اليه واعمل للنار بقدر صبرك عليها');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 15, NULL, 'Work for this world as much as you will live in it, and work for the life hereafter as much as you wish to live there, and work for Allah the Exalted as much as you are in need of Him, and work toward avoiding the Fire as much as you can bear its pain. "', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'work for this world as much as you will live in it and work for the life hereafter as much as you wish to live there and work for allah the exalted as much as you are in need of him and work toward avoiding the fire as much as you can bear its pain', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 16, NULL, 'The Prophet ﷺ spoke these words to one of his companions.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'the prophet ﷺ spoke these words to one of his companions', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 17, NULL, 'My dear beloved son! It is plain from this hadith that you do not need a vast store of knowledge, for studying and acquiring knowledge beyond one''s personal obligation is فَرْضُ كِفَايَة, a communal obligation discharged once enough members of the Muslim community fulfill it; but if no one discharges it, the whole community bears the sin of that neglect. Give your closest attention to what follows, so that you may attain a high degree of certitude.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'my dear beloved son it is plain from this hadith that you do not need a vast store of knowledge for studying and acquiring knowledge beyond ones personal obligation is فرض كفايه a communal obligation discharged once enough members of the muslim community fulfill it but if no one discharges it the whole community bears the sin of that neglect give your closest attention to what follows so that you may attain a high degree of certitude', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'worship is obedience, nothing less', '4. Worship Is Obedience, Nothing Less', 18, NULL, 'Editorial note (tradition-grounded). A related teaching preserved in this tradition clarifies the three terms this chapter turns on. Mujahadah, which the chapter calls the sword against the lower self, comes from a root meaning to strain and exert oneself to the very limit; its inner form is precisely this disciplined struggle against one''s own appetites and laziness. Sunnah, the Prophet''s established way, derives from a root meaning to lay down and smooth a well-trodden course — the path he set that others now follow, which is why conforming to it is presented here as the whole substance of obedience. And halal, invoked when the chapter defends lawful intimacy, comes from a root meaning to untie or loosen a knot: it is what has been loosed from prohibition, an act "unknotted" and therefore permitted. Reading these roots together shows why worship, for this author, is finally another name for keeping to the loosened, well-trodden way.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 4 worship is obedience nothing less', 'editorial note tradition grounded a related teaching preserved in this tradition clarifies the three terms this chapter turns on mujahadah which the chapter calls the sword against the lower self comes from a root meaning to strain and exert oneself to the very limit its inner form is precisely this disciplined struggle against ones own appetites and laziness sunnah the prophets established way derives from a root meaning to lay down and smooth a well trodden course the path he set that others now follow which is why conforming to it is presented here as the whole substance of obedience and halal invoked when the chapter defends lawful intimacy comes from a root meaning to untie or loosen a knot it is what has been loosed from prohibition an act unknotted and therefore permitted reading these roots together shows why worship for this author is finally another name for keeping to the loosened well trodden way', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 0, NULL, 'Consider now, my dear son, a story told of the seekers and disciples — those who strive to rise toward God and toward the light by cleansing the soul of every evil desire and inclination, under a master who has himself reached that purity and that high station before Allah through the training, guidance, and nearness of his own teacher. Among the disciples of Shaykh Shafeeq al-Balkhi was one such seeker, Hatim bin Ism, who through Shaykh Shafeeq''s training and guidance later became a great master in his own right. One day Shaykh Shafeeq said to him:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'consider now my dear son a story told of the seekers and disciples those who strive to rise toward god and toward the light by cleansing the soul of every evil desire and inclination under a master who has himself reached that purity and that high station before allah through the training guidance and nearness of his own teacher among the disciples of shaykh shafeeq al balkhi was one such seeker hatim bin ism who through shaykh shafeeqs training and guidance later became a great master in his own right one day shaykh shafeeq said to him', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 1, NULL, '"O Hatim! How long have you kept my company, close at my side, listening to what I have taught?"', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'o hatim how long have you kept my company close at my side listening to what i have taught', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 2, NULL, 'Hatim replied: "Thirty-three years."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'hatim replied thirty three years', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 3, NULL, 'Shaykh Shafeeq asked: "In all that long time, what benefit have you drawn from me?"', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'shaykh shafeeq asked in all that long time what benefit have you drawn from me', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 4, NULL, 'Hatim answered: "I have gained eight benefits from you."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'hatim answered i have gained eight benefits from you', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 5, NULL, 'At this Shaykh Shafeeq said: "To Allah we belong, and to Him we shall return." Then he said: "O Hatim! I have spent my whole life teaching and training, and you have gained only eight benefits from me?"', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'at this shaykh shafeeq said to allah we belong and to him we shall return then he said o hatim i have spent my whole life teaching and training and you have gained only eight benefits from me', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 6, NULL, 'Hatim replied: "O my teacher! If you ask me for the truth, then the truth is exactly as I have said. I need nothing more, for these eight benefits of knowledge are enough for me, and I am certain that through them I will attain salvation and success in this life and in the next."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'hatim replied o my teacher if you ask me for the truth then the truth is exactly as i have said i need nothing more for these eight benefits of knowledge are enough for me and i am certain that through them i will attain salvation and success in this life and in the next', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 7, NULL, 'Shaykh Shafeeq said: "Very well. Tell me, then — what are those eight benefits?"', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'shaykh shafeeq said very well tell me then what are those eight benefits', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 8, NULL, 'These, then, are the benefits as Hatim bin Ism (رض) related them.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'these then are the benefits as hatim bin ism رض related them', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 9, NULL, '"O my teacher! The first benefit is this. I looked at the people of this world and saw that every one of them has a beloved. Yet the beloveds of worldly people are such that some go with their lovers only until the illness that brings on death, some until death itself, and some only as far as the edge of the grave. Once the burial is done, they all turn back and leave; not one of them goes down into the grave to keep the dead company in its darkness, its loneliness, its narrowness, and its terror. I thought about this and said to myself: the only true beloved is one that goes down with a person into the grave, that stays there beside him, that keeps him company, that lights up the grave, and that remains his companion on the Day of Judgment and through every station he passes after burial until that Day. And I saw that the only beloved with these qualities is a person''s own righteous deeds. Once I understood this and resolved upon it, I took my righteous deeds for my beloved, so that they will go down with me into my grave when I die, will keep me company there, will become a shining lamp within my grave, will be my companion through every stage of the life to come, and will never part from me."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'o my teacher the first benefit is this i looked at the people of this world and saw that every one of them has a beloved yet the beloveds of worldly people are such that some go with their lovers only until the illness that brings on death some until death itself and some only as far as the edge of the grave once the burial is done they all turn back and leave not one of them goes down into the grave to keep the dead company in its darkness its loneliness its narrowness and its terror i thought about this and said to myself the only true beloved is one that goes down with a person into the grave that stays there beside him that keeps him company that lights up the grave and that remains his companion on the day of judgment and through every station he passes after burial until that day and i saw that the only beloved with these qualities is a persons own righteous deeds once i understood this and resolved upon it i took my righteous deeds for my beloved so that they will go down with me into my grave when i die will keep me company there will become a shining lamp within my grave will be my companion through every stage of the life to come and will never part from me', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 10, NULL, 'Shaykh Shafeeq said: "Well done, O Hatim! You have said a most excellent thing. Now tell me the second benefit."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'shaykh shafeeq said well done o hatim you have said a most excellent thing now tell me the second benefit', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 11, NULL, '"O my teacher! The second benefit is this. I glanced at the people of the world and saw that everyone chases the pleasures and cravings of the نَفْس, the lower self that urges toward evil, and that everyone is a slave to its desires. Seeing this, I reflected on this holy verse:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'o my teacher the second benefit is this i glanced at the people of the world and saw that everyone chases the pleasures and cravings of the نفس the lower self that urges toward evil and that everyone is a slave to its desires seeing this i reflected on this holy verse', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 12, NULL, 'Sayingوَأَمَّا مَنْ خَافَ مَقَامَ رَبِّهِ وَنَهَى النَّفْسَ عَنِ الْهَوَى * فَإِنَّ الْجَنَّةَ هِيَ الْمَأْوَى"But as for the one who has stood in awe of his Sustainer''s Presence and restrained his inner self from base and evil desires, Paradise will be the place of rest." (Quran, Chapter 79, Verses 40 to 41)', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'sayingواما من خاف مقام ربه ونهي النفس عن الهوي فان الجنه هي الماوي but as for the one who has stood in awe of his sustainers presence and restrained his inner self from base and evil desires paradise will be the place of rest quran chapter 79 verses 40 to 41', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 13, NULL, '"I became certain that the Wise Quran is true and that the Book of Allah is the truth. So I set myself firmly against my نَفْس and resolved to oppose it. I disciplined it so thoroughly that I refused to satisfy any of its cravings, until at last it found its peace and its contentment in the worship of Allah, the Exalted."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'i became certain that the wise quran is true and that the book of allah is the truth so i set myself firmly against my نفس and resolved to oppose it i disciplined it so thoroughly that i refused to satisfy any of its cravings until at last it found its peace and its contentment in the worship of allah the exalted', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 14, NULL, 'Shaykh Shafeeq said: "May Allah, the Exalted, bless you in your good deeds. Now tell me the third benefit."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'shaykh shafeeq said may allah the exalted bless you in your good deeds now tell me the third benefit', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 15, NULL, '"O my teacher! The third benefit is this. When I looked at the people, I saw that every one of them, through great pain and hard labor, was busy heaping up the goods of this passing world — its money, wealth, and possessions — and rejoiced greatly at having gathered so much of it. But then I reflected on this holy verse of the generous Quran:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'o my teacher the third benefit is this when i looked at the people i saw that every one of them through great pain and hard labor was busy heaping up the goods of this passing world its money wealth and possessions and rejoiced greatly at having gathered so much of it but then i reflected on this holy verse of the generous quran', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 16, NULL, 'An-Nahl: 96مَا عِنْدَكُمْ يَنْفَدُ وَمَا عِنْدَ اللهِ بَاقٍ"All that is with you is bound to come to an end, whereas all that is with Allah is everlasting." (Quran, Chapter 16, Verse 96)', '', NULL, 'An-Nahl: 96', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'an nahl 96ما عندكم ينفد وما عند الله باق all that is with you is bound to come to an end whereas all that is with allah is everlasting quran chapter 16 verse 96', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 17, NULL, '"So whatever material goods I had gathered in this world, I spent entirely in the Way of Allah, distributing them among the pious poor and the needy, so that they would be laid up with Allah, the Exalted, as my trust, and would become a source of immense good and of ransom — that is, of salvation — in the life to come."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'so whatever material goods i had gathered in this world i spent entirely in the way of allah distributing them among the pious poor and the needy so that they would be laid up with allah the exalted as my trust and would become a source of immense good and of ransom that is of salvation in the life to come', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 18, NULL, 'Shaykh Shafeeq said: "O Hatim! May Allah reward you for your spending in His Way. You have said an excellent thing and done an excellent thing. Now tell me the fourth benefit."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'shaykh shafeeq said o hatim may allah reward you for your spending in his way you have said an excellent thing and done an excellent thing now tell me the fourth benefit', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 19, NULL, '"O my teacher! The fourth benefit is this. I looked at the people of the world and saw that some of them imagine greatness and honor to lie in belonging to a large and powerful nation or tribe, and so they take pride in their tribe. Others imagine that greatness comes from an abundance of wealth, possessions, family, and kin, and so they boast of their riches and their children. Others reckon their greatness and honor to lie in violence — in beating, killing, murder, and plunder — and take pride in that. And others hold that their greatness lies in lavish spending, counting such extravagance a badge of honor and boasting of it. But I reflected on this verse, in which Allah, the Exalted, says:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'o my teacher the fourth benefit is this i looked at the people of the world and saw that some of them imagine greatness and honor to lie in belonging to a large and powerful nation or tribe and so they take pride in their tribe others imagine that greatness comes from an abundance of wealth possessions family and kin and so they boast of their riches and their children others reckon their greatness and honor to lie in violence in beating killing murder and plunder and take pride in that and others hold that their greatness lies in lavish spending counting such extravagance a badge of honor and boasting of it but i reflected on this verse in which allah the exalted says', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 20, NULL, 'Al-Hujurat: 13إِنَّ أَكْرَمَكُمْ عِنْدَ اللهِ أَتْقَاكُمْ"Verily, the noblest of you in the sight of Allah is the one most deeply conscious of Him, most in awe and fear of Him." (Quran, Chapter 49, Verse 13)', '', NULL, 'Al-Hujurat: 13', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'al hujurat 13ان اكرمكم عند الله اتقاكم verily the noblest of you in the sight of allah is the one most deeply conscious of him most in awe and fear of him quran chapter 49 verse 13', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 21, NULL, '"This alone is correct; this alone is the truth. The notions of the people of the world are false guesses and nothing more. So I chose تَقْوَى — that reverent consciousness, awe, and fear of Allah — so that I might be counted among those of the highest rank in the sight of Allah, the Exalted."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'this alone is correct this alone is the truth the notions of the people of the world are false guesses and nothing more so i chose تقوي that reverent consciousness awe and fear of allah so that i might be counted among those of the highest rank in the sight of allah the exalted', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 22, NULL, 'Shaykh Shafeeq said: "O Hatim! May Allah be pleased with you. You have said a most excellent thing. Now tell me the fifth benefit."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'shaykh shafeeq said o hatim may allah be pleased with you you have said a most excellent thing now tell me the fifth benefit', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 23, NULL, '"O my teacher! The fifth benefit is this. I looked at people finding fault with one another and speaking ill of one another. I found that they did so out of envy, jealousy, and enmity, and that the root of it all was rivalry over greatness, grandeur, possessions, wealth, and knowledge. So I reflected on this verse of the Holy Quran:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'o my teacher the fifth benefit is this i looked at people finding fault with one another and speaking ill of one another i found that they did so out of envy jealousy and enmity and that the root of it all was rivalry over greatness grandeur possessions wealth and knowledge so i reflected on this verse of the holy quran', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 24, NULL, 'Az-Zukhruf: 32نَحْنُ قَسَمْنَا بَيْنَهُمْ مَعِيشَتَهُمْ فِي الْحَيَاةِ الدُّنْيَا"It is We who apportion among them their means of livelihood, their sustenance, in the life of this world." (Quran, Chapter 43, Verse 32)', '', NULL, 'Az-Zukhruf: 32', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'az zukhruf 32نحن قسمنا بينهم معيشتهم في الحياه الدنيا it is we who apportion among them their means of livelihood their sustenance in the life of this world quran chapter 43 verse 32', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 25, NULL, '"I saw then that Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, has fixed all possessions and all ranks from the very beginning of creation, and that no one has any hand or say in the matter. So I stopped competing with anyone or envying anyone, and I rested content with Allah''s apportioning of worldly bounty and with His decree, living at peace with all of creation by giving up every jealousy and enmity toward His creatures."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'i saw then that allah the blessed and exalted has fixed all possessions and all ranks from the very beginning of creation and that no one has any hand or say in the matter so i stopped competing with anyone or envying anyone and i rested content with allahs apportioning of worldly bounty and with his decree living at peace with all of creation by giving up every jealousy and enmity toward his creatures', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 26, NULL, 'Shaykh Shafeeq said: "O Hatim! You speak the truth and you do what is right. Now tell me the sixth benefit."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'shaykh shafeeq said o hatim you speak the truth and you do what is right now tell me the sixth benefit', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 27, NULL, '"O my teacher! The sixth benefit is this. When I looked at people, I saw that every one of them holds enmity toward some other for one reason or another. Then I reflected on this verse:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'o my teacher the sixth benefit is this when i looked at people i saw that every one of them holds enmity toward some other for one reason or another then i reflected on this verse', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 28, NULL, 'Fatir: 6إِنَّ الشَّيْطَانَ لَكُمْ عَدُوٌّ فَاتَّخِذُوهُ عَدُوًّا"Verily, Shaytan is your enemy, so treat him as your enemy." (Quran, Chapter 35, Verse 6)', '', NULL, 'Fatir: 6', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'fatir 6ان الشيطان لكم عدو فاتخذوه عدوا verily shaytan is your enemy so treat him as your enemy quran chapter 35 verse 6', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 29, NULL, '"After that I became certain that the words of Allah are the truth, and that I should hold no enmity toward any person. From that time on I took Shaytan alone for my enemy and refused to obey any of his promptings. Instead I cultivated obedience to Allah, followed the commandments of Allah, the Exalted, and gave myself wholly to His worship and service."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'after that i became certain that the words of allah are the truth and that i should hold no enmity toward any person from that time on i took shaytan alone for my enemy and refused to obey any of his promptings instead i cultivated obedience to allah followed the commandments of allah the exalted and gave myself wholly to his worship and service', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 30, NULL, 'Shaykh Shafeeq said: "O Hatim! You have done an excellent thing and said an excellent thing. Now tell me the seventh benefit."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'shaykh shafeeq said o hatim you have done an excellent thing and said an excellent thing now tell me the seventh benefit', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 31, NULL, '"O my teacher! The seventh benefit is this. I looked at people and saw that every one of them is consumed by the search for livelihood and sustenance, wearing himself out in that pursuit. In this race many no longer tell apart the Halal, what is permitted, from the Haram, what is forbidden, and they debase themselves by chasing after doubtful and forbidden earnings. Then I reflected on this verse:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'o my teacher the seventh benefit is this i looked at people and saw that every one of them is consumed by the search for livelihood and sustenance wearing himself out in that pursuit in this race many no longer tell apart the halal what is permitted from the haram what is forbidden and they debase themselves by chasing after doubtful and forbidden earnings then i reflected on this verse', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 32, NULL, 'Hud: 6وَمَا مِنْ دَابَّةٍ فِي الْأَرْضِ إِلَّا عَلَى اللهِ رِزْقُهَا"And there is no creature that moves upon the earth whose sustenance is not the responsibility of Allah." (Quran, Chapter 11, Verse 6)', '', NULL, 'Hud: 6', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'hud 6وما من دابه في الارض الا علي الله رزقها and there is no creature that moves upon the earth whose sustenance is not the responsibility of allah quran chapter 11 verse 6', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 33, NULL, '"I became certain that the Wise Quran is true and correct, and that I too am one of those moving creatures of God upon the earth. So I gave myself to the worship of Allah and held firmly to the certainty that He would bring my sustenance to me, for He has promised to provide it."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'i became certain that the wise quran is true and correct and that i too am one of those moving creatures of god upon the earth so i gave myself to the worship of allah and held firmly to the certainty that he would bring my sustenance to me for he has promised to provide it', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 34, NULL, 'Shaykh Shafeeq said: "You have done an excellent thing and said an excellent thing. Now tell me the eighth benefit."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'shaykh shafeeq said you have done an excellent thing and said an excellent thing now tell me the eighth benefit', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 35, NULL, '"O my teacher! The eighth benefit is this. When I looked at people, I found that everyone''s reliance and trust rests upon something or someone: some place their trust in their possessions, and some lean upon other people. Then I pondered this holy verse, in which Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, says:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'o my teacher the eighth benefit is this when i looked at people i found that everyones reliance and trust rests upon something or someone some place their trust in their possessions and some lean upon other people then i pondered this holy verse in which allah the blessed and exalted says', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 36, NULL, 'At-Talaq: 3وَمَنْ يَتَوَكَّلْ عَلَى اللهِ فَهُوَ حَسْبُهُ إِنَّ اللهَ بَالِغُ أَمْرِهِ قَدْ جَعَلَ اللهُ لِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدْرًا"And whoever places his trust in Allah, He alone is sufficient for him. Verily, Allah brings His command to pass; indeed, Allah has set a measure for all things." (Quran, Chapter 65, Verse 3)', '', NULL, 'At-Talaq: 3', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'at talaq 3ومن يتوكل علي الله فهو حسبه ان الله بالغ امره قد جعل الله لكل شيء قدرا and whoever places his trust in allah he alone is sufficient for him verily allah brings his command to pass indeed allah has set a measure for all things quran chapter 65 verse 3', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 37, NULL, '"So I placed my trust in Allah, and He is sufficient for me, فَهُوَ حَسْبِي وَنِعْمَ الْوَكِيلُ, He is sufficient for me and the best of trustees. From then on I gave myself over to complete reliance upon Allah in every matter."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'so i placed my trust in allah and he is sufficient for me فهو حسبي ونعم الوكيل he is sufficient for me and the best of trustees from then on i gave myself over to complete reliance upon allah in every matter', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'eight lessons from thirty-three years', '5. Eight Lessons from Thirty-Three Years', 38, NULL, 'When Shaykh Shafeeq heard these eight benefits, he said: "O Hatim! May Allah grant you His اَلتَّوْفِيق — that is, may He enable you to do righteous deeds and to walk firmly upon His Path. You have conveyed most excellent things. I have looked into the Tawrat, the Injil, the Zabur, and the praiseworthy Criterion between right and wrong, the Holy Quran, and I have found that all four of these Books of God turn upon these very eight benefits. Whoever acts upon these eight principles is as one who has acted upon all four holy Books."', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 5 eight lessons from thirty three years', 'when shaykh shafeeq heard these eight benefits he said o hatim may allah grant you his التوفيق that is may he enable you to do righteous deeds and to walk firmly upon his path you have conveyed most excellent things i have looked into the tawrat the injil the zabur and the praiseworthy criterion between right and wrong the holy quran and i have found that all four of these books of god turn upon these very eight benefits whoever acts upon these eight principles is as one who has acted upon all four holy books', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'finding a true guide', '6. Finding a True Guide', 0, NULL, 'My dear beloved son, you now know from these traditions that you have no need of a great store of knowledge. Let me return, then, to my explanation and set out for you the inward conditions required of the student who seeks the Path of God.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 6 finding a true guide', 'my dear beloved son you now know from these traditions that you have no need of a great store of knowledge let me return then to my explanation and set out for you the inward conditions required of the student who seeks the path of god', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'finding a true guide', '6. Finding a True Guide', 1, NULL, 'The fifth condition binding upon the seeker on the Path of Allah, the Exalted, is that he must have a شَيْخُ الْكَامِل, a perfected spiritual guide, who leads him, draws the evil qualities and habits out of him, and sets noble ones in their place. This spiritual training is like the work of a farmer tending his crop. When he sees useless weeds that have sprung up among the grain, he pulls them out and throws them away, and then he waters the crop and feeds it so that it grows well and ripens to good quality. In the same way, at every stage of the journey the traveler on the Path of Allah has no remedy and no solution except through a مُرْشِد اَلْكَامِل, a true and perfected spiritual guide. For Allah, the Exalted, sent the Prophet Muhammad (صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ) to His servants so that he might be an illumined guide upon the Way of Allah and bring the people to that Way. When the Messenger of Allah (صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ) departed from this world, he appointed his deputies and successors in his place, so that they might stand as proofs of the Way of Allah, the Exalted, until the Day of Judgment.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 6 finding a true guide', 'the fifth condition binding upon the seeker on the path of allah the exalted is that he must have a شيخ الكامل a perfected spiritual guide who leads him draws the evil qualities and habits out of him and sets noble ones in their place this spiritual training is like the work of a farmer tending his crop when he sees useless weeds that have sprung up among the grain he pulls them out and throws them away and then he waters the crop and feeds it so that it grows well and ripens to good quality in the same way at every stage of the journey the traveler on the path of allah has no remedy and no solution except through a مرشد الكامل a true and perfected spiritual guide for allah the exalted sent the prophet muhammad صلي الله عليه وسلم to his servants so that he might be an illumined guide upon the way of allah and bring the people to that way when the messenger of allah صلي الله عليه وسلم departed from this world he appointed his deputies and successors in his place so that they might stand as proofs of the way of allah the exalted until the day of judgment', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'finding a true guide', '6. Finding a True Guide', 2, NULL, 'The seeker of God, therefore, must have a perfected spiritual guide, a شَيْخُ الْكَامِل, who, for the sake of walking the Path of Allah, stands as an illumined proof and deputy of the Prophet Muhammad (صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ).', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 6 finding a true guide', 'the seeker of god therefore must have a perfected spiritual guide a شيخ الكامل who for the sake of walking the path of allah stands as an illumined proof and deputy of the prophet muhammad صلي الله عليه وسلم', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'finding a true guide', '6. Finding a True Guide', 3, NULL, 'The heir of the Messenger of Allah (صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ) whom one takes as his Shaykh must be a scholar, yet not every scholar is fit to be a شَيْخُ الْكَامِل. For this work of guiding people spiritually, purifying their souls, and helping them advance upon the Way of Allah, only a person who possesses certain particular qualities is truly qualified. I set down some of these qualities briefly here, so that no foolish or misguided person may falsely claim to be a Shaykh. Such impostors are many, and their only aim is to deceive people for material gain, doing great harm to this vital dimension of Islam and leading many astray.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 6 finding a true guide', 'the heir of the messenger of allah صلي الله عليه وسلم whom one takes as his shaykh must be a scholar yet not every scholar is fit to be a شيخ الكامل for this work of guiding people spiritually purifying their souls and helping them advance upon the way of allah only a person who possesses certain particular qualities is truly qualified i set down some of these qualities briefly here so that no foolish or misguided person may falsely claim to be a shaykh such impostors are many and their only aim is to deceive people for material gain doing great harm to this vital dimension of islam and leading many astray', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'finding a true guide', '6. Finding a True Guide', 4, NULL, 'I say that a man is fit to be a Shaykh only when, having turned away from love of the world, love of honor, and love of worldly rank, he has taken اَلْبَيْعَة, a solemn covenant with Allah made upon the hand of a Shaykh, pledging to turn away from evil inwardly and outwardly, to excel upon the Path of God by obeying His commandments and keeping away from His prohibitions, and to strive to purify the soul in pursuit of God''s nearness and approval, with a perfected spiritual guide, who in his turn has taken اَلْبَيْعَة with his own perfected spiritual guide, and so on, so that this spiritual chain reaches all the way back to the Messenger of Allah (صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ). Such a man must have passed through every manner of اَلرِّيَاضَة and الْمُجَاهَدَة, spiritual struggles and disciplines, and must have obeyed every commandment of the Prophet Muhammad (صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ). He must eat little, sleep little, and pray abundantly, performing the five obligatory daily prayers as well as the supererogatory prayers; he must fast often and give generously in charity and in the Way of God. Within his character, inward and outward alike, there must dwell every praiseworthy quality: patience, gratitude, certitude in Allah, generosity, contentment, trustworthiness, tolerance, seriousness of purpose, humility, obedience to Allah, truthfulness, modesty and reserve, dignity, tranquility, and all virtues of their kind. He must have received a light from among the lights of the Messenger of Allah (صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ), so that every evil quality, miserliness, jealousy, enmity, worldly expectation, anger, and rebellion, has been purged from within him; and he must rely upon no one for knowledge except for the knowledge we receive from the Messenger of Allah (صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ).', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 6 finding a true guide', 'i say that a man is fit to be a shaykh only when having turned away from love of the world love of honor and love of worldly rank he has taken البيعه a solemn covenant with allah made upon the hand of a shaykh pledging to turn away from evil inwardly and outwardly to excel upon the path of god by obeying his commandments and keeping away from his prohibitions and to strive to purify the soul in pursuit of gods nearness and approval with a perfected spiritual guide who in his turn has taken البيعه with his own perfected spiritual guide and so on so that this spiritual chain reaches all the way back to the messenger of allah صلي الله عليه وسلم such a man must have passed through every manner of الرياضه and المجاهده spiritual struggles and disciplines and must have obeyed every commandment of the prophet muhammad صلي الله عليه وسلم he must eat little sleep little and pray abundantly performing the five obligatory daily prayers as well as the supererogatory prayers he must fast often and give generously in charity and in the way of god within his character inward and outward alike there must dwell every praiseworthy quality patience gratitude certitude in allah generosity contentment trustworthiness tolerance seriousness of purpose humility obedience to allah truthfulness modesty and reserve dignity tranquility and all virtues of their kind he must have received a light from among the lights of the messenger of allah صلي الله عليه وسلم so that every evil quality miserliness jealousy enmity worldly expectation anger and rebellion has been purged from within him and he must rely upon no one for knowledge except for the knowledge we receive from the messenger of allah صلي الله عليه وسلم', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'finding a true guide', '6. Finding a True Guide', 5, NULL, 'These qualities, which are among the marks that distinguish the perfected spiritual guides who lead others upon the Path of God, are what make a man worthy to be a deputy and heir of the Messenger of Allah (صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَيْهِ وَسَلَّمَ). To follow such guides is the correct way.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 6 finding a true guide', 'these qualities which are among the marks that distinguish the perfected spiritual guides who lead others upon the path of god are what make a man worthy to be a deputy and heir of the messenger of allah صلي الله عليه وسلم to follow such guides is the correct way', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'finding a true guide', '6. Finding a True Guide', 6, NULL, 'Such spiritual guides are exceedingly hard to find. But if a man has been granted this wealth and blessing, so that he has found such a guide and the guide has admitted him among his disciples, his Mureeds, then that disciple must keep both outward and inward respect, honor, and proper etiquette toward his spiritual guide.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 6 finding a true guide', 'such spiritual guides are exceedingly hard to find but if a man has been granted this wealth and blessing so that he has found such a guide and the guide has admitted him among his disciples his mureeds then that disciple must keep both outward and inward respect honor and proper etiquette toward his spiritual guide', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'finding a true guide', '6. Finding a True Guide', 7, NULL, 'The outward etiquettes toward one''s spiritual guide are these. The disciple should not argue with the guide, and if a dispute arises and the disciple imagines that the guide has overlooked something, he should not raise an objection. He should not remain at the place of prayer at all times; rather, when the time for prayer arrives, he should go to the place of prayer, perform the prayer, then roll up his prayer mat and leave. He should not perform lengthy supererogatory prayers before the Shaykh after each obligatory prayer. Whatever the perfected guide commands, the disciple should obey to the best of his ability. And it is of the greatest importance to understand that a true spiritual guide can never and will never command his disciple to do anything forbidden by the Shariah, the law of Allah. The false impostors, who lack the qualities I have named, may by contrast command their disciples to do things that are Haram.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 6 finding a true guide', 'the outward etiquettes toward ones spiritual guide are these the disciple should not argue with the guide and if a dispute arises and the disciple imagines that the guide has overlooked something he should not raise an objection he should not remain at the place of prayer at all times rather when the time for prayer arrives he should go to the place of prayer perform the prayer then roll up his prayer mat and leave he should not perform lengthy supererogatory prayers before the shaykh after each obligatory prayer whatever the perfected guide commands the disciple should obey to the best of his ability and it is of the greatest importance to understand that a true spiritual guide can never and will never command his disciple to do anything forbidden by the shariah the law of allah the false impostors who lack the qualities i have named may by contrast command their disciples to do things that are haram', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'finding a true guide', '6. Finding a True Guide', 8, NULL, 'The inward etiquettes toward one''s spiritual guide are these. Whatever the disciple hears outwardly from the Murshid, he should not entertain even the least doubt in his heart concerning the Shaykh''s words or deeds, lest he be counted among the hypocrites. And if he cannot maintain this inner sincerity, then he should withdraw from the company of his spiritual guide until his inward state comes into harmony with his outward.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 6 finding a true guide', 'the inward etiquettes toward ones spiritual guide are these whatever the disciple hears outwardly from the murshid he should not entertain even the least doubt in his heart concerning the shaykhs words or deeds lest he be counted among the hypocrites and if he cannot maintain this inner sincerity then he should withdraw from the company of his spiritual guide until his inward state comes into harmony with his outward', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'finding a true guide', '6. Finding a True Guide', 9, NULL, 'The sixth condition is that the disciple, the seeker of truth, guards himself against the deceptions of the نَفْس, the lower self, an inward and subtle presence invisible to the outward eye yet plainly perceived by one who possesses inner sight, which commands a man to evil by whispering into his heart. This is possible only if the seeker abandons the gatherings of the ill-charactered and the ignorant. In this way the dominion of Shaytan over the heart is brought to an end, and every Satanic influence is uprooted entirely. This Satanic company may come from among mankind or from among the Jinn, an unseen creation of God made of fire, to whose kind Shaytan belongs and from among whom he recruits his followers.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 6 finding a true guide', 'the sixth condition is that the disciple the seeker of truth guards himself against the deceptions of the نفس the lower self an inward and subtle presence invisible to the outward eye yet plainly perceived by one who possesses inner sight which commands a man to evil by whispering into his heart this is possible only if the seeker abandons the gatherings of the ill charactered and the ignorant in this way the dominion of shaytan over the heart is brought to an end and every satanic influence is uprooted entirely this satanic company may come from among mankind or from among the jinn an unseen creation of god made of fire to whose kind shaytan belongs and from among whom he recruits his followers', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'finding a true guide', '6. Finding a True Guide', 10, NULL, 'The seventh condition is that in every circumstance the seeker should prefer humility and freedom from greed over wealth, and should embrace contentment and satisfaction with the Will of Allah. These seven conditions are essential for every traveler upon the Path of Allah, the Exalted, and for every seeker of truth.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 6 finding a true guide', 'the seventh condition is that in every circumstance the seeker should prefer humility and freedom from greed over wealth and should embrace contentment and satisfaction with the will of allah these seven conditions are essential for every traveler upon the path of allah the exalted and for every seeker of truth', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'finding a true guide', '6. Finding a True Guide', 11, NULL, 'Editorial note (tradition-grounded). The word Shaykh, as the wider tradition traces its root, begins simply as "old man" — one who has grown old and become an elder — and ripens into "master," the one whose years have matured into authority worth following. This chapter''s insistence that not every scholar is fit to be a Shaykh al-Kamil turns on exactly that ripening: age or learning alone does not make the master, but a life purified until it carries authority a seeker can safely follow. The same tradition marks the boundary the true guide never crosses. The term Haram joins two ideas under one limit — to forbid, and to make sacred — so that what is Haram lies across a line drawn around something sacred. This clarifies the chapter''s test: a perfected guide will never command what is Haram, because he never asks the disciple to step over a boundary God has set as holy.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 6 finding a true guide', 'editorial note tradition grounded the word shaykh as the wider tradition traces its root begins simply as old man one who has grown old and become an elder and ripens into master the one whose years have matured into authority worth following this chapters insistence that not every scholar is fit to be a shaykh al kamil turns on exactly that ripening age or learning alone does not make the master but a life purified until it carries authority a seeker can safely follow the same tradition marks the boundary the true guide never crosses the term haram joins two ideas under one limit to forbid and to make sacred so that what is haram lies across a line drawn around something sacred this clarifies the chapters test a perfected guide will never command what is haram because he never asks the disciple to step over a boundary god has set as holy', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 0, NULL, 'You have asked me what تَصَوُّف is. Know that تَصَوُّف rests upon two qualities. The first is that the servant obeys Allah, living by the Shariah — the outward law of Islam, with its obligations, its commands, and its prohibitions. The second is that the seeker is compassionate toward all of Allah''s creation and does them good, and this is not possible until he has first cleansed his soul of every base quality and then adorned it with every noble one. Whoever holds fast to the Shariah and truly wants the good and the welfare of all people is rightly called a Sufi — and this is what the word really means, unlike the corrupted senses in which it is so often used today. To be loyal and obedient to Allah is to give up your own share of happiness for the sake of His command. To be sincere toward all people is this: that you do not drag others toward what you want, but bring yourself toward what they want, so long as they do not transgress the Shariah. So the seeker keeps no bond for a private end of his own, but serves selflessly, giving himself to the good of all — on the condition that this good stays within the Divine law.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'you have asked me what تصوف is know that تصوف rests upon two qualities the first is that the servant obeys allah living by the shariah the outward law of islam with its obligations its commands and its prohibitions the second is that the seeker is compassionate toward all of allahs creation and does them good and this is not possible until he has first cleansed his soul of every base quality and then adorned it with every noble one whoever holds fast to the shariah and truly wants the good and the welfare of all people is rightly called a sufi and this is what the word really means unlike the corrupted senses in which it is so often used today to be loyal and obedient to allah is to give up your own share of happiness for the sake of his command to be sincere toward all people is this that you do not drag others toward what you want but bring yourself toward what they want so long as they do not transgress the shariah so the seeker keeps no bond for a private end of his own but serves selflessly giving himself to the good of all on the condition that this good stays within the divine law', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 1, NULL, 'You have asked me what servitude is. Servitude consists of three things.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'you have asked me what servitude is servitude consists of three things', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 2, NULL, 'The first is to keep the command of the Shariah.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'the first is to keep the command of the shariah', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 3, NULL, 'The second is to be content with the decree and the destiny that Allah, the Exalted, appoints, and with the portion He allots.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'the second is to be content with the decree and the destiny that allah the exalted appoints and with the portion he allots', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 4, NULL, 'The third is to give up your own pleasure in seeking the pleasure of Allah, the Exalted, resting content and glad in His Will and His Power.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'the third is to give up your own pleasure in seeking the pleasure of allah the exalted resting content and glad in his will and his power', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 5, NULL, 'You have asked me what reliance upon Allah is — what تَوَكُّل is. Know that تَوَكُّل is to make your faith firm in what Allah, the Exalted, has promised: to believe with certainty that whatever He has decreed for you will reach you without fail, though everyone in the world should strive to turn it away, and that whatever He has not written for you will never reach you, though the whole world should come to help you get it.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'you have asked me what reliance upon allah is what توكل is know that توكل is to make your faith firm in what allah the exalted has promised to believe with certainty that whatever he has decreed for you will reach you without fail though everyone in the world should strive to turn it away and that whatever he has not written for you will never reach you though the whole world should come to help you get it', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 6, NULL, 'You have asked me what sincerity is — what إِخْلَاص is. Know that إِخْلَاص is that all your deeds are for Allah, the Exalted, alone, that your heart takes no comfort in people''s praise and pays no heed to their blame. Nothing you do should be done for show, for اَلرِّيَاء. Your heart should be neither gladdened by people''s admiration nor grieved by their criticism. Know that اَلرِّيَاء is born of magnifying the creation. Its cure is to see all people as subject to the Power of Allah, and to reckon them like lifeless stones that have no power to bring you either ease or hardship, so that you may be freed from displaying yourself to them. As long as you reckon them to hold power and will, اَلرِّيَاء will not leave you.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'you have asked me what sincerity is what اخلاص is know that اخلاص is that all your deeds are for allah the exalted alone that your heart takes no comfort in peoples praise and pays no heed to their blame nothing you do should be done for show for الرياء your heart should be neither gladdened by peoples admiration nor grieved by their criticism know that الرياء is born of magnifying the creation its cure is to see all people as subject to the power of allah and to reckon them like lifeless stones that have no power to bring you either ease or hardship so that you may be freed from displaying yourself to them as long as you reckon them to hold power and will الرياء will not leave you', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 7, NULL, 'My dear son, the rest of your questions are of two kinds. Some of them I have already set down in books I have written, so look for their answers there; and some are such that to write their answers is not permitted. Act upon what you know, and through that acting there will be unveiled to you what you do not yet know.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'my dear son the rest of your questions are of two kinds some of them i have already set down in books i have written so look for their answers there and some are such that to write their answers is not permitted act upon what you know and through that acting there will be unveiled to you what you do not yet know', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 8, NULL, 'My dear son, from this day on, do not ask me about what is difficult for you except through the tongue of the heart, for Allah, the Exalted, has said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'my dear son from this day on do not ask me about what is difficult for you except through the tongue of the heart for allah the exalted has said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 9, NULL, 'Saying(وَلَوْ اَلُهُمْ صَبَرُوا حَتَّى تَخْرُجَ اِلَيْهِمْ لَكَانَ خَيْرًا لَهُمْ * الحجرات: ٥)"Had those people been patient until you yourself came out to them, it would have been better for them." (Quran, Chapter 49, Verse 5)', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'saying ولو الهم صبروا حتي تخرج اليهم لكان خيرا لهم الحجرات ٥ had those people been patient until you yourself came out to them it would have been better for them quran chapter 49 verse 5', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 10, NULL, 'And accept the counsel of Khidr (اَلْخِضْر) (ع) when he said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'and accept the counsel of khidr الخضر ع when he said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 11, NULL, 'Saying(فَلاَ تَسْئَلْنِي عَنْ شَيْءٍ حَتَّى أُحْدِثَ لَكَ مِنْهُ ذِكْرًا * الكهف: ٧٠)"Do not question me about anything until I myself bring it up to you and explain it." (Quran, Chapter 18, Verse 70)', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'saying فلا تسيلني عن شيء حتي احدث لك منه ذكرا الكهف ٧٠ do not question me about anything until i myself bring it up to you and explain it quran chapter 18 verse 70', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 12, NULL, 'Do not be hasty until a thing''s time arrives; then it will be unveiled to you and you will see it, for Allah, the Exalted, has said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'do not be hasty until a things time arrives then it will be unveiled to you and you will see it for allah the exalted has said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 13, NULL, 'Saying(سَأُرِيكُّمْ آيَاتٍ فَلاَ تَسْتَعْجِلوُنِ * الْأَنْبِيَاءُ: ٣٧)"I will show you My signs, so do not seek to hasten them." (Quran, Chapter 21, Verse 37)', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'saying ساريكم ايات فلا تستعجلون الانبياء ٣٧ i will show you my signs so do not seek to hasten them quran chapter 21 verse 37', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 14, NULL, 'So do not ask me before the time. And be certain that you will not arrive except by traveling the road, for Allah, the Exalted, has said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'so do not ask me before the time and be certain that you will not arrive except by traveling the road for allah the exalted has said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 15, NULL, 'Saying(آَوَ لَمْ يَسِيرُوا فِيِ اْلاَرْضِ فَيَنْظُرُوا * فاطر: ٤٤)"Have they not traveled through the earth and seen?" (Quran, Chapter 35, Verse 44)', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'saying او لم يسيروا في الارض فينظروا فاطر ٤٤ have they not traveled through the earth and seen quran chapter 35 verse 44', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 16, NULL, 'My dear son, by Allah, if you travel you will see wonders at every stage — so give of your very soul, for the head of this whole matter is the giving of the soul. As Zun Noon al-Misri (may Allah have mercy on him) said to one of his students:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'my dear son by allah if you travel you will see wonders at every stage so give of your very soul for the head of this whole matter is the giving of the soul as zun noon al misri may allah have mercy on him said to one of his students', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'sufism, servitude, trust, sincerity', '7. Sufism, Servitude, Trust, Sincerity', 17, NULL, 'SayingZun Noon al-Misri«إِنْ قَدَرْتَ عَلَىٰ بَذْلِ الرُّوحِ فَتَعَالَ، وَ إِلَّا فَلَا تَشْتَغِلْ بِتُرَّهَاتِ الصُّوفِيَّةِ.»"If you are able to give of your soul, then come; and if not, do not busy yourself with the idle talk of the Sufis."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 7 sufism servitude trust sincerity', 'sayingzun noon al misri ان قدرت علي بذل الروح فتعال و الا فلا تشتغل بترهات الصوفيه if you are able to give of your soul then come and if not do not busy yourself with the idle talk of the sufis', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 0, NULL, 'My dear son, I will bring my teaching to a close now with eight counsels. Take them from me, so that on the Day of Judgment your knowledge does not stand against you. Four of these eight are things you must do, and four are things you must not do. Let me begin with the four you are to leave.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'my dear son i will bring my teaching to a close now with eight counsels take them from me so that on the day of judgment your knowledge does not stand against you four of these eight are things you must do and four are things you must not do let me begin with the four you are to leave', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 1, NULL, 'The first is this: as far as you are able, do not dispute with anyone over any question, for such disputes carry many hidden harms, and their sin outweighs their benefit. Debate is the fountainhead of every blameworthy trait — ostentation, envy, arrogance, resentment, enmity, self-display, and the like. Yes, if a question arises between you and another person or group, and your one wish in it is that the truth should come to light and not be lost, then inquiry is permitted. But that intention has two marks. The first is that it makes no difference to you whether the truth appears upon your own tongue or upon the tongue of another. The second is that you would rather examine the matter in private than before an audience.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'the first is this as far as you are able do not dispute with anyone over any question for such disputes carry many hidden harms and their sin outweighs their benefit debate is the fountainhead of every blameworthy trait ostentation envy arrogance resentment enmity self display and the like yes if a question arises between you and another person or group and your one wish in it is that the truth should come to light and not be lost then inquiry is permitted but that intention has two marks the first is that it makes no difference to you whether the truth appears upon your own tongue or upon the tongue of another the second is that you would rather examine the matter in private than before an audience', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 2, NULL, 'Now hear a benefit I set down for you here. To ask about difficult matters is to bring the disease of the heart before the physician, and the answer he gives is his effort to cure that disease. The ignorant are the sick, whose hearts are diseased, and the scholars are the physicians. The deficient scholar cannot heal well, and even the accomplished scholar does not treat every patient. He treats only the one in whom he hopes the cure will be accepted and reform will follow. When the malady is chronic or beyond remedy and will not accept treatment, the physician''s skill lies in saying, "This one will not accept a cure, so do not trouble yourself with treating him" — for to do so is to waste one''s life.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'now hear a benefit i set down for you here to ask about difficult matters is to bring the disease of the heart before the physician and the answer he gives is his effort to cure that disease the ignorant are the sick whose hearts are diseased and the scholars are the physicians the deficient scholar cannot heal well and even the accomplished scholar does not treat every patient he treats only the one in whom he hopes the cure will be accepted and reform will follow when the malady is chronic or beyond remedy and will not accept treatment the physicians skill lies in saying this one will not accept a cure so do not trouble yourself with treating him for to do so is to waste ones life', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 3, NULL, 'Then know that the disease of ignorance is of four kinds. One accepts the cure; the other three do not.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'then know that the disease of ignorance is of four kinds one accepts the cure the other three do not', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 4, NULL, 'As for the three that do not accept the cure: the first is the person whose question and objection spring from his envy and his hatred. However finely, however clearly, however eloquently you answer him, it adds nothing to him but more hatred, enmity, and envy. The way, then, is not to trouble yourself with answering him. As it has been said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'as for the three that do not accept the cure the first is the person whose question and objection spring from his envy and his hatred however finely however clearly however eloquently you answer him it adds nothing to him but more hatred enmity and envy the way then is not to trouble yourself with answering him as it has been said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 5, NULL, 'Verseكُلُّ الْعَدَاوَةِ قَدْ تُرْجَى إِزَالَتُهَاإِلَّا عَدَاوَةَ مَنْ عَادَاكَ عَنْ حَسَدٍ"Every enmity may be hoped to be removed, save the enmity of one who bears you enmity out of envy."', '', NULL, 'Verse', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'verseكل العداوه قد ترجي ازالتهاالا عداوه من عاداك عن حسد every enmity may be hoped to be removed save the enmity of one who bears you enmity out of envy', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 6, NULL, 'You should turn away from him and leave him to his disease. Allah, the Exalted, said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'you should turn away from him and leave him to his disease allah the exalted said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 7, NULL, 'Sayingفَاَعْرِضْ عَنْ مَنْ تَوَلَّى عَنْ ذِكْرِنَا وَلَمْ يُرِدْ اِلاَّ الْحَيَوَةَ الدُّنَا (النَّجْم: ٢٩)"So turn away from him who turns his back on Our remembrance and desires nothing but the life of this world."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'sayingفاعرض عن من تولي عن ذكرنا ولم يرد الا الحيوه الدنا النجم ٢٩ so turn away from him who turns his back on our remembrance and desires nothing but the life of this world', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 8, NULL, 'With everything he says and does, the envier kindles a fire in the standing crop of his own deeds, for the Prophet ﷺ said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'with everything he says and does the envier kindles a fire in the standing crop of his own deeds for the prophet ﷺ said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 9, NULL, 'Sayingالْحَسَدُ يَأْكُلُ الْحَسَنَاتِ كَمَا تَأْكُلُ النَّارُ الْحَطَبَ"Envy devours good deeds as fire devours firewood."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'sayingالحسد ياكل الحسنات كما تاكل النار الحطب envy devours good deeds as fire devours firewood', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 10, NULL, 'The second is the one whose malady is foolishness, and this too does not accept the cure. As Isa (ع) said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'the second is the one whose malady is foolishness and this too does not accept the cure as isa ع said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 11, NULL, 'SayingIsa (ع)إِنِّي مَا عَجَزْتُ عَنْ إِحْيَاءِ الْمَوْتَى وَقَدْ عَجَزْتُ عَنْ مُعَالَجَةِ الْأَحْمَقِ"I was not powerless to bring the dead to life, yet I was powerless to treat the fool."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'sayingisa ع اني ما عجزت عن احياء الموتي وقد عجزت عن معالجه الاحمق i was not powerless to bring the dead to life yet i was powerless to treat the fool', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 12, NULL, 'This is a man who applies himself to seeking knowledge for a short while, learns some small portion of the rational and religious sciences, and then, out of his foolishness, questions and objects against the great scholar who has spent his life in those sciences. This fool does not realize his own ignorance; he imagines that what is obscure to him is obscure to the great scholar as well. Since he cannot grasp even this much, his question springs from foolishness, and you should not trouble yourself with answering him.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'this is a man who applies himself to seeking knowledge for a short while learns some small portion of the rational and religious sciences and then out of his foolishness questions and objects against the great scholar who has spent his life in those sciences this fool does not realize his own ignorance he imagines that what is obscure to him is obscure to the great scholar as well since he cannot grasp even this much his question springs from foolishness and you should not trouble yourself with answering him', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 13, NULL, 'The third is one who is seeking guidance, who traces whatever he does not understand of the words of the great back to the shortcoming of his own understanding, and who asks in order to benefit; yet he is dull and does not perceive realities. It is not fitting to trouble yourself with answering him either, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'the third is one who is seeking guidance who traces whatever he does not understand of the words of the great back to the shortcoming of his own understanding and who asks in order to benefit yet he is dull and does not perceive realities it is not fitting to trouble yourself with answering him either for the messenger of allah ﷺ said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 14, NULL, 'Sayingنحن معاشر الْأَنْبِيَاءُ أمرنا أن نكلّم النّاس على قدر عقولهم"We, the company of the prophets, have been commanded to speak to people according to the measure of their minds."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'sayingنحن معاشر الانبياء امرنا ان نكلم الناس علي قدر عقولهم we the company of the prophets have been commanded to speak to people according to the measure of their minds', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 15, NULL, 'As for the disease that does accept the cure: it is the one who is seeking guidance, intelligent and understanding, who is not overpowered by envy, anger, or love of appetite, rank, or wealth, and who is a seeker of the straight path — whose question and objection do not spring from envy, obstinacy, or a wish to test you. This one accepts the cure, so you may trouble yourself with answering his question. Indeed, it is your duty to answer him.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'as for the disease that does accept the cure it is the one who is seeking guidance intelligent and understanding who is not overpowered by envy anger or love of appetite rank or wealth and who is a seeker of the straight path whose question and objection do not spring from envy obstinacy or a wish to test you this one accepts the cure so you may trouble yourself with answering his question indeed it is your duty to answer him', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 16, NULL, 'The second thing you must leave is to guard against becoming a preacher and an admonisher, for there is much affliction in it — unless you first act upon what you say and then admonish others by it. Reflect on what was said to Isa:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'the second thing you must leave is to guard against becoming a preacher and an admonisher for there is much affliction in it unless you first act upon what you say and then admonish others by it reflect on what was said to isa', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 17, NULL, 'Sayingيَا ابْنَ مَرْيَمَ عِظْ نَفْسَكَ فَإِنِ اتَّعَظْتَ فَعِظِ النَّاسَ وَإِلَّا فَاسْتَحِ مِنْ رَبِّكَ"O son of Maryam, admonish your own soul; then, if it takes the admonition, admonish the people, and if not, be ashamed before your Lord."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'sayingيا ابن مريم عظ نفسك فان اتعظت فعظ الناس والا فاستح من ربك o son of maryam admonish your own soul then if it takes the admonition admonish the people and if not be ashamed before your lord', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 18, NULL, 'And if you are tried with this work, then beware of two traits. The first is affectation in speech — ornate phrases, allusions, extravagant claims, verses, and poetry — for Allah, the Exalted, detests the affected, and affectation that oversteps the bound points to the ruin of the inward and the heedlessness of the heart. The meaning of admonition is that the servant should recall the fire of the Hereafter and his own failing in the service of his Creator; that he should reflect upon his past life, wasted in what did not concern him; and that he should reflect upon the obstacles before him — the possibility that his faith may not be sound at the end, his state under the seizing hand of the Angel of Death, whether he will be able to answer Munkar and Nakir (مُنْكَر وَنَكِير), his condition at the Resurrection and its stations, and whether he will cross the Bridge in safety or fall into the abyss. Let the remembrance of these things stay in his heart until it drives him from his rest. This boiling of these fires and this wailing over these calamities is what is called admonition. Its aim is to make the people know these things, to alert them to their negligence and their excess, and to open their eyes to the defects of their own souls, so that the heat of these fires may touch those present in the gathering and these calamities may grieve them, that they may make up their past life as far as they can and grieve over the days that passed without the obedience of Allah, the Exalted. This whole matter, upon this path, is what is called preaching.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'and if you are tried with this work then beware of two traits the first is affectation in speech ornate phrases allusions extravagant claims verses and poetry for allah the exalted detests the affected and affectation that oversteps the bound points to the ruin of the inward and the heedlessness of the heart the meaning of admonition is that the servant should recall the fire of the hereafter and his own failing in the service of his creator that he should reflect upon his past life wasted in what did not concern him and that he should reflect upon the obstacles before him the possibility that his faith may not be sound at the end his state under the seizing hand of the angel of death whether he will be able to answer munkar and nakir منكر ونكير his condition at the resurrection and its stations and whether he will cross the bridge in safety or fall into the abyss let the remembrance of these things stay in his heart until it drives him from his rest this boiling of these fires and this wailing over these calamities is what is called admonition its aim is to make the people know these things to alert them to their negligence and their excess and to open their eyes to the defects of their own souls so that the heat of these fires may touch those present in the gathering and these calamities may grieve them that they may make up their past life as far as they can and grieve over the days that passed without the obedience of allah the exalted this whole matter upon this path is what is called preaching', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 19, NULL, 'It is as if you saw that a flood had rushed upon a man''s house while he and his household were inside it, so you cry out, "Beware, beware! Flee from the flood!"', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'it is as if you saw that a flood had rushed upon a mans house while he and his household were inside it so you cry out beware beware flee from the flood', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 20, NULL, 'Sayingالْحَذَرَ الْحَذَرَ، فِرُّوا مِنَ السَّيْلِ', '', 'الْحَذَرَ الْحَذَرَ، فِرُّوا مِنَ السَّيْلِ', 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'sayingالحذر الحذر فروا من السيل', 'الحذر الحذر فروا من السيل');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 21, NULL, 'In such a state, would your heart wish to inform the master of the house of his danger with labored phrases, subtleties, and allusions? Not in the least. Such is the condition of the preacher, and he ought to shun all of that.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'in such a state would your heart wish to inform the master of the house of his danger with labored phrases subtleties and allusions not in the least such is the condition of the preacher and he ought to shun all of that', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 22, NULL, 'The second trait is that your concern in your preaching should not be that the people cry out in your gathering, or display ecstasy, or tear their garments, so that it may be said, "What an excellent gathering this was!" — for all of that is inclination toward this world, and it is born of heedlessness. Rather, your resolve and your concern should be to call the people from this world to the Hereafter, from disobedience to obedience, from greed to abstinence, from miserliness to generosity, from doubt to certainty, from heedlessness to wakefulness, and from delusion to God-fearing. You should make the Hereafter beloved to them and this world hateful, and teach them the knowledge of worship and abstinence. Do not deceive them with the generosity of Allah, the Exalted, and His mercy, for the prevailing tendency in their natures is to swerve from the way of the Law, to strive after what does not please Allah, and to be content with base traits of character. So cast dread into their hearts, alarm them, and warn them of the terrors that face them, that perhaps the qualities of their inward may change and the dealings of their outward be transformed, and that they may show eagerness and desire for obedience and a turning away from disobedience.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'the second trait is that your concern in your preaching should not be that the people cry out in your gathering or display ecstasy or tear their garments so that it may be said what an excellent gathering this was for all of that is inclination toward this world and it is born of heedlessness rather your resolve and your concern should be to call the people from this world to the hereafter from disobedience to obedience from greed to abstinence from miserliness to generosity from doubt to certainty from heedlessness to wakefulness and from delusion to god fearing you should make the hereafter beloved to them and this world hateful and teach them the knowledge of worship and abstinence do not deceive them with the generosity of allah the exalted and his mercy for the prevailing tendency in their natures is to swerve from the way of the law to strive after what does not please allah and to be content with base traits of character so cast dread into their hearts alarm them and warn them of the terrors that face them that perhaps the qualities of their inward may change and the dealings of their outward be transformed and that they may show eagerness and desire for obedience and a turning away from disobedience', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 23, NULL, 'This is the path of preaching and sincere counsel. Every sermon that is not thus is a bane upon the one who speaks and the one who hears. Indeed, it has been said that such a speaker is a ghoul and a devil who leads the creation away from the path and destroys them, so that they are forced to flee from him — for the corruption that this speaker works in their religion, not even the devil can work its like. And whoever has the hand and the power is obliged to bring him down from the pulpits of admonition and to restrain him from what he has undertaken, for this is part of enjoining the right and forbidding the wrong.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'this is the path of preaching and sincere counsel every sermon that is not thus is a bane upon the one who speaks and the one who hears indeed it has been said that such a speaker is a ghoul and a devil who leads the creation away from the path and destroys them so that they are forced to flee from him for the corruption that this speaker works in their religion not even the devil can work its like and whoever has the hand and the power is obliged to bring him down from the pulpits of admonition and to restrain him from what he has undertaken for this is part of enjoining the right and forbidding the wrong', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 24, NULL, 'The third thing you must leave is to keep from mixing with commanders and rulers, and even from looking upon them, for to see them, to sit with them, and to mingle with them is a great affliction. And if you are tried with that, then keep clear of praising them and lauding them, for Allah, the Exalted, is angered when the corrupt and the oppressor are praised. It has been said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'the third thing you must leave is to keep from mixing with commanders and rulers and even from looking upon them for to see them to sit with them and to mingle with them is a great affliction and if you are tried with that then keep clear of praising them and lauding them for allah the exalted is angered when the corrupt and the oppressor are praised it has been said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 25, NULL, 'Sayingإِنَّ اللَّهَ تَعَالَىٰ يَغْضَبُ إِذَا مُدِحَ الْفَاسِقُ وَالظَّالِمُ، وَمَنْ دَعَا لِطُولِ بَقَائِهِمْ فَقَدْ أَحَبَّ أَنْ يُعْصَىٰ اللَّهُ فِي أَرْضِهِ"Allah, the Exalted, is angered when the corrupt and the oppressor are praised; and whoever prays for the length of their stay has loved that Allah be disobeyed in His earth."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'sayingان الله تعالي يغضب اذا مدح الفاسق والظالم ومن دعا لطول بقايهم فقد احب ان يعصي الله في ارضه allah the exalted is angered when the corrupt and the oppressor are praised and whoever prays for the length of their stay has loved that allah be disobeyed in his earth', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 26, NULL, 'The fourth thing you must leave is that you accept nothing of the gifts and presents of the rulers, even if you know them to be from what is lawful, for coveting from them corrupts the religion — since from it are born flattery, the guarding of their side, and agreement with them in their injustice, and all of this is corruption in the religion. The least of its harm is that, when you accept their gifts and profit from their world, you come to love them; and whoever loves someone necessarily loves the length of his life and his continuance; and in loving the continuance of the oppressor there is a desire for oppression upon the servants of Allah, the Exalted, and a desire for the ruin of the world. What thing, then, could be more harmful than this to the religion and the final end?', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'the fourth thing you must leave is that you accept nothing of the gifts and presents of the rulers even if you know them to be from what is lawful for coveting from them corrupts the religion since from it are born flattery the guarding of their side and agreement with them in their injustice and all of this is corruption in the religion the least of its harm is that when you accept their gifts and profit from their world you come to love them and whoever loves someone necessarily loves the length of his life and his continuance and in loving the continuance of the oppressor there is a desire for oppression upon the servants of allah the exalted and a desire for the ruin of the world what thing then could be more harmful than this to the religion and the final end', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 27, NULL, 'Beware, beware, that the seduction of the devils deceive you, or the saying of some people to you that it is better and more fitting for you to take the dinar and the dirham from them and distribute them among the poor and the destitute — since they would only spend it in corruption and disobedience, and your spending upon the weak among the people is better than their spending. For that accursed one has cut the throats of many people with this very whispering. We have treated this at length in the Revival of the Sciences of Religion, so seek it there.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'beware beware that the seduction of the devils deceive you or the saying of some people to you that it is better and more fitting for you to take the dinar and the dirham from them and distribute them among the poor and the destitute since they would only spend it in corruption and disobedience and your spending upon the weak among the people is better than their spending for that accursed one has cut the throats of many people with this very whispering we have treated this at length in the revival of the sciences of religion so seek it there', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 28, NULL, 'Now for the four things it is fitting that you should do.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'now for the four things it is fitting that you should do', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 29, NULL, 'The first is that you make your dealing with Allah, the Exalted, such that, were your own servant to deal with you by it, you would be content with it from him, would not be vexed with him, and would not be angered. And whatever you would not accept for yourself from your hired servant, do not accept it either for Allah, the Exalted, who is your true Master.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'the first is that you make your dealing with allah the exalted such that were your own servant to deal with you by it you would be content with it from him would not be vexed with him and would not be angered and whatever you would not accept for yourself from your hired servant do not accept it either for allah the exalted who is your true master', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 30, NULL, 'The second is that whatever you do with people, make it such as you would be pleased to receive from them, for the faith of a servant is not complete until he loves for all people what he loves for himself. As it has been said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'the second is that whatever you do with people make it such as you would be pleased to receive from them for the faith of a servant is not complete until he loves for all people what he loves for himself as it has been said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 31, NULL, 'Sayingلَا يَكْمُلُ إِيمَانُ عَبْدٍ حَتَّى يُحِبَّ لِسَائِرِ النَّاسِ مَا يُحِبُّ لِنَفْسِهِ"A servant''s faith is not complete until he loves for all people what he loves for himself."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'sayingلا يكمل ايمان عبد حتي يحب لساير الناس ما يحب لنفسه a servants faith is not complete until he loves for all people what he loves for himself', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 32, NULL, 'The third is that when you read knowledge or study it, your knowledge should be such as reforms your heart and purifies your soul. Consider it as though you knew that your life would last no longer than a week; then of necessity you would not busy yourself in it with the science of jurisprudence, ethics, principles, theology, and the like, for you would know that these sciences would not avail you. Rather, you would busy yourself with watching over the heart, with knowing the qualities of the soul, with turning away from the attachments of this world, with purifying your soul of blameworthy traits, and with the love of Allah, the Exalted, His worship, and the adorning of yourself with beautiful qualities. And let no day and night pass over a servant but that it is possible his death may fall within it.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'the third is that when you read knowledge or study it your knowledge should be such as reforms your heart and purifies your soul consider it as though you knew that your life would last no longer than a week then of necessity you would not busy yourself in it with the science of jurisprudence ethics principles theology and the like for you would know that these sciences would not avail you rather you would busy yourself with watching over the heart with knowing the qualities of the soul with turning away from the attachments of this world with purifying your soul of blameworthy traits and with the love of allah the exalted his worship and the adorning of yourself with beautiful qualities and let no day and night pass over a servant but that it is possible his death may fall within it', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 33, NULL, 'My dear son, hear from me another word and reflect upon it, that you may find deliverance. If you were told that the sultan would come to visit you after a week, I know that in that span you would busy yourself with nothing but putting right whatever you knew the sultan''s gaze would fall upon — the garments, the body, the house, the furnishings, and the rest. Now consider what I am pointing you toward, for you are a man of understanding, and a single word suffices the discerning. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'my dear son hear from me another word and reflect upon it that you may find deliverance if you were told that the sultan would come to visit you after a week i know that in that span you would busy yourself with nothing but putting right whatever you knew the sultans gaze would fall upon the garments the body the house the furnishings and the rest now consider what i am pointing you toward for you are a man of understanding and a single word suffices the discerning the messenger of allah ﷺ said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 34, NULL, 'Prophetic traditionإِنَّ اللَّهَ لَا يَنْظُرُ إِلَى صُوَرِكُمْ وَلَا إِلَى أَعْمَالِكُمْ وَلَكِنْ يَنْظُرُ إِلَى قُلُوبِكُمْ وَنِيَّاتِكُمْ"Allah does not look at your forms nor at your deeds, but He looks at your hearts and your intentions."', '', NULL, 'Prophetic tradition', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'prophetic traditionان الله لا ينظر الي صوركم ولا الي اعمالكم ولكن ينظر الي قلوبكم ونياتكم allah does not look at your forms nor at your deeds but he looks at your hearts and your intentions', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 35, NULL, 'If you desire the knowledge of the states of the heart, then look into the Ihya and the others of my writings, for this knowledge is an individual obligation, while other knowledge is a communal obligation, save for the measure by which the ordinances of Allah, the Exalted, are discharged. And He it is who gives you the ability to attain it.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'if you desire the knowledge of the states of the heart then look into the ihya and the others of my writings for this knowledge is an individual obligation while other knowledge is a communal obligation save for the measure by which the ordinances of allah the exalted are discharged and he it is who gives you the ability to attain it', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 36, NULL, 'The fourth is that you should not gather of this world more than a year''s sufficiency, as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would set that aside for some of his chambers, and said:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'the fourth is that you should not gather of this world more than a years sufficiency as the messenger of allah ﷺ would set that aside for some of his chambers and said', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 37, NULL, 'Sayingأَللَّهُمَّ اجْعَلْ قُوتَ آلِ مُحَمَّدٍ كَفَافًا"O Allah, make the provision of the family of Muhammad just enough."', '', NULL, 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'sayingاللهم اجعل قوت ال محمد كفافا o allah make the provision of the family of muhammad just enough', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'four to avoid, four to embrace', '8. Four to Avoid, Four to Embrace', 38, NULL, 'He did not set it aside for all his chambers; rather he set it aside for the one in whose heart he knew there was a weakness. As for her who was a possessor of certainty, he would set aside for her no more than the provision of a day, or half of it.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 8 four to avoid four to embrace', 'he did not set it aside for all his chambers rather he set it aside for the one in whose heart he knew there was a weakness as for her who was a possessor of certainty he would set aside for her no more than the provision of a day or half of it', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'a prayer for the road', '9. A Prayer for the Road', 0, NULL, 'My dear son, I have now answered in this book every question you put to me. Gather your resolve, act upon all that it contains, and do not forget me in your supplications, your دُعَاء.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 9 a prayer for the road', 'my dear son i have now answered in this book every question you put to me gather your resolve act upon all that it contains and do not forget me in your supplications your دعاء', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'a prayer for the road', '9. A Prayer for the Road', 1, NULL, 'You also asked me to write out a prayer that you might keep upon your tongue at all times. For that, turn to the supplications preserved in the books of hadith, the traditions of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, and above all in the اَلصِّحَاحُ اَلسِّتَّة, the six most trustworthy of his collections, and commit them to memory. Many supplications have likewise come down to us through the أَهْلُ الْبَيْت, the family of the Prophet ﷺ; seek them there as well. And recite this supplication in particular at all times, and especially in the moments that follow each of your prayers:', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 9 a prayer for the road', 'you also asked me to write out a prayer that you might keep upon your tongue at all times for that turn to the supplications preserved in the books of hadith the traditions of the prophet muhammad ﷺ and above all in the الصحاح السته the six most trustworthy of his collections and commit them to memory many supplications have likewise come down to us through the اهل البيت the family of the prophet ﷺ seek them there as well and recite this supplication in particular at all times and especially in the moments that follow each of your prayers', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'a prayer for the road', '9. A Prayer for the Road', 2, NULL, 'Saying«أَلَّهُمَّ إِنِّي أَسْأَلُكَ مِنَ النِّعْمَةِ تَمَامَهَا، وَ مِنَ الْعِصْمَةِ دَوَامُهَا، وَ مِنَ الرَّحْمَةِ شُمُولُهَا، وَ مِنَ الْعَافِيَةِ حُصُولُهَا، و من العيش ارغده، وَ مِنَ الْعُمْرِ أَسْعَدُهُ، وَ مِنَ الْإِحْسَانِ أَتَمَّهُ، وَمِنَ الْإِنْعَامِ أَعَمُّهُ، وَ مِنَ الْفَضْلِ أَعْذَبُهُ، وَ مِنَ اللُّطْفِ أَنْفَعُهُأَلَّهُمَّ كُنْ لَنَا وَ لَا تَكُنْ عَلَيْنَاأَلَّهُمَّ اخْتِمْ بِالسَّعَادَةِ آجَالَنَا. وَ حَقِّقْ بِالزِّيَادَةِ آمَالَنَا، و اقرن بالعافية غدوّنا و آصالنا، وَ اجْعَلْ إِلَىٰ رَحْمَتِكَ مَصِيرَنَا وَ مَآلَنَا، و اصبب سجال عفوك على ذنوبنا، وَ مَنَّ عَلَيْنَا بِإِصْلَاحِ عُيُوبِنَا، وَ اجْعَلِ التَّقْوَى زَادَنَا، وَ فِي دِينِكَ إِجْتِهَادُنَا، وعليك توكّلنا و اعتمادنا. اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عَلَى نَهْجِ الْإِسْتِقَامَةِ، وَأَعِذْنَا فِي الدُّنْيَا مِنْ مُوجِبَاتِ النَّدَامَةِ يَوْمَ الْقِيَامَةِ، و خفّف عنّا ثقل الأوزار، و ارزقنا عيشة الأبرار، واكفنا ما اهمّنا في هذه الدّار و في تلك الدّار واصرف عنّا شرّ الأشرار و كيد الفجّار و اعتق رقابنا و رقاب آبائنا و أمّهاتنا و إخواننا وأخواتنا من النّار، بِرَحْمَتِكَ يَا عَزِيزُ يَا غَفَّارُ، يَا كَرِيمُ يَا سَتَّارُ يَا خَالِقَ اللَّيْلِ وَ النَّهَارِ خَلِّصْنَا مِنْ هَمِّ الدُّنْيَا وَ عَذَابِ الْقَبْرِ وَ النَّارِ يَا عَلِيمُ يَا جَبَّارُ، يا الله، يا ألله، يا ألله، بِرَحْمَتِكَ يَا أَرْحَمَ الرَّاحِمِينَ، و يا أول الأوّلین، و يا آخر الآخرین، وَ يَا ذَا الْقُوَّةِ الْمَتِينُ، وَيَا رَاحِمَ الْمَسَاكِينِ، وَ يَا أَرْحَمَ الرَّاحِمِينَ، لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا أَنْتَ سُبْحَانَكَ إِنِّي كُنْتُ مِنَ الظَّالِمِينَ. وَ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَ آلِهِ وَصَحْبِهِ أَجْمَعِينَ، وَ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ.»', '', '«أَلَّهُمَّ إِنِّي أَسْأَلُكَ مِنَ النِّعْمَةِ تَمَامَهَا، وَ مِنَ الْعِصْمَةِ دَوَامُهَا، وَ مِنَ الرَّحْمَةِ شُمُولُهَا، وَ مِنَ الْعَافِيَةِ حُصُولُهَا، و من العيش ارغده، وَ مِنَ الْعُمْرِ أَسْعَدُهُ، وَ مِنَ الْإِحْسَانِ أَتَمَّهُ، وَمِنَ الْإِنْعَامِ أَعَمُّهُ، وَ مِنَ الْفَضْلِ أَعْذَبُهُ، وَ مِنَ اللُّطْفِ أَنْفَعُهُ أَلَّهُمَّ كُنْ لَنَا وَ لَا تَكُنْ عَلَيْنَا أَلَّهُمَّ اخْتِمْ بِالسَّعَادَةِ آجَالَنَا. وَ حَقِّقْ بِالزِّيَادَةِ آمَالَنَا، و اقرن بالعافية غدوّنا و آصالنا، وَ اجْعَلْ إِلَىٰ رَحْمَتِكَ مَصِيرَنَا وَ مَآلَنَا، و اصبب سجال عفوك على ذنوبنا، وَ مَنَّ عَلَيْنَا بِإِصْلَاحِ عُيُوبِنَا، وَ اجْعَلِ التَّقْوَى زَادَنَا، وَ فِي دِينِكَ إِجْتِهَادُنَا، وعليك توكّلنا و اعتمادنا. اللَّهُمَّ ثَبِّتْنَا عَلَى نَهْجِ الْإِسْتِقَامَةِ، وَأَعِذْنَا فِي الدُّنْيَا مِنْ مُوجِبَاتِ النَّدَامَةِ يَوْمَ الْقِيَامَةِ، و خفّف عنّا ثقل الأوزار، و ارزقنا عيشة الأبرار، واكفنا ما اهمّنا في هذه الدّار و في تلك الدّار واصرف عنّا شرّ الأشرار و كيد الفجّار و اعتق رقابنا و رقاب آبائنا و أمّهاتنا و إخواننا وأخواتنا من النّار، بِرَحْمَتِكَ يَا عَزِيزُ يَا غَفَّارُ، يَا كَرِيمُ يَا سَتَّارُ يَا خَالِقَ اللَّيْلِ وَ النَّهَارِ خَلِّصْنَا مِنْ هَمِّ الدُّنْيَا وَ عَذَابِ الْقَبْرِ وَ النَّارِ يَا عَلِيمُ يَا جَبَّارُ، يا الله، يا ألله، يا ألله، بِرَحْمَتِكَ يَا أَرْحَمَ الرَّاحِمِينَ، و يا أول الأوّلین، و يا آخر الآخرین، وَ يَا ذَا الْقُوَّةِ الْمَتِينُ، وَيَا رَاحِمَ الْمَسَاكِينِ، وَ يَا أَرْحَمَ الرَّاحِمِينَ، لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا أَنْتَ سُبْحَانَكَ إِنِّي كُنْتُ مِنَ الظَّالِمِينَ. وَ صَلَّى اللَّهُ عَلَى سَيِّدِنَا مُحَمَّدٍ وَ آلِهِ وَصَحْبِهِ أَجْمَعِينَ، وَ الْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ الْعَالَمِينَ.»', 'Saying', NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 9 a prayer for the road', 'saying الهم اني اسالك من النعمه تمامها و من العصمه دوامها و من الرحمه شمولها و من العافيه حصولها و من العيش ارغده و من العمر اسعده و من الاحسان اتمه ومن الانعام اعمه و من الفضل اعذبه و من اللطف انفعهالهم كن لنا و لا تكن عليناالهم اختم بالسعاده اجالنا و حقق بالزياده امالنا و اقرن بالعافيه غدونا و اصالنا و اجعل الي رحمتك مصيرنا و مالنا و اصبب سجال عفوك علي ذنوبنا و من علينا باصلاح عيوبنا و اجعل التقوي زادنا و في دينك اجتهادنا وعليك توكلنا و اعتمادنا اللهم ثبتنا علي نهج الاستقامه واعذنا في الدنيا من موجبات الندامه يوم القيامه و خفف عنا ثقل الاوزار و ارزقنا عيشه الابرار واكفنا ما اهمنا في هذه الدار و في تلك الدار واصرف عنا شر الاشرار و كيد الفجار و اعتق رقابنا و رقاب اباينا و امهاتنا و اخواننا واخواتنا من النار برحمتك يا عزيز يا غفار يا كريم يا ستار يا خالق الليل و النهار خلصنا من هم الدنيا و عذاب القبر و النار يا عليم يا جبار يا الله يا الله يا الله برحمتك يا ارحم الراحمين و يا اول الاولين و يا اخر الاخرين و يا ذا القوه المتين ويا راحم المساكين و يا ارحم الراحمين لا اله الا انت سبحانك اني كنت من الظالمين و صلي الله علي سيدنا محمد و اله وصحبه اجمعين و الحمد لله رب العالمين', 'الهم اني اسالك من النعمه تمامها و من العصمه دوامها و من الرحمه شمولها و من العافيه حصولها و من العيش ارغده و من العمر اسعده و من الاحسان اتمه ومن الانعام اعمه و من الفضل اعذبه و من اللطف انفعه الهم كن لنا و لا تكن علينا الهم اختم بالسعاده اجالنا و حقق بالزياده امالنا و اقرن بالعافيه غدونا و اصالنا و اجعل الي رحمتك مصيرنا و مالنا و اصبب سجال عفوك علي ذنوبنا و من علينا باصلاح عيوبنا و اجعل التقوي زادنا و في دينك اجتهادنا وعليك توكلنا و اعتمادنا اللهم ثبتنا علي نهج الاستقامه واعذنا في الدنيا من موجبات الندامه يوم القيامه و خفف عنا ثقل الاوزار و ارزقنا عيشه الابرار واكفنا ما اهمنا في هذه الدار و في تلك الدار واصرف عنا شر الاشرار و كيد الفجار و اعتق رقابنا و رقاب اباينا و امهاتنا و اخواننا واخواتنا من النار برحمتك يا عزيز يا غفار يا كريم يا ستار يا خالق الليل و النهار خلصنا من هم الدنيا و عذاب القبر و النار يا عليم يا جبار يا الله يا الله يا الله برحمتك يا ارحم الراحمين و يا اول الاولين و يا اخر الاخرين و يا ذا القوه المتين ويا راحم المساكين و يا ارحم الراحمين لا اله الا انت سبحانك اني كنت من الظالمين و صلي الله علي سيدنا محمد و اله وصحبه اجمعين و الحمد لله رب العالمين');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'a prayer for the road', '9. A Prayer for the Road', 3, NULL, 'O my Allah! I ask of You the fullness and perfection of Your blessings; the permanence of inward and outward purity; the all-encompassing breadth of Your mercy; the attainment of health; increase in my sustenance; happiness in my life; true and lasting success in this life; the completion of noble qualities; from Your rewards, the most abundant reward; from Your plenitude, the sweetest plenitude; from Your favors, the most plentiful favor; from all deeds, the deed that is best; from all knowledge, the knowledge that is most beneficial; and from all sustenance, the sustenance that is most wholesome.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 9 a prayer for the road', 'o my allah i ask of you the fullness and perfection of your blessings the permanence of inward and outward purity the all encompassing breadth of your mercy the attainment of health increase in my sustenance happiness in my life true and lasting success in this life the completion of noble qualities from your rewards the most abundant reward from your plenitude the sweetest plenitude from your favors the most plentiful favor from all deeds the deed that is best from all knowledge the knowledge that is most beneficial and from all sustenance the sustenance that is most wholesome', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'a prayer for the road', '9. A Prayer for the Road', 4, NULL, 'O my Allah! Be on our side and grant us benefit; do not burden us, but keep us from harm. O my Allah! Grant us salvation at our death; set our deeds right; keep us safe through Your days and Your nights; extend Your mercy to us in our homes and in our possessions; veil our sins and faults with Your forgiveness; do us the favor of correcting all our shortcomings, inward and outward; and grant us confidence and reliance upon You.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 9 a prayer for the road', 'o my allah be on our side and grant us benefit do not burden us but keep us from harm o my allah grant us salvation at our death set our deeds right keep us safe through your days and your nights extend your mercy to us in our homes and in our possessions veil our sins and faults with your forgiveness do us the favor of correcting all our shortcomings inward and outward and grant us confidence and reliance upon you', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'a prayer for the road', '9. A Prayer for the Road', 5, NULL, 'O my Allah! Grant us steadfastness and perseverance in our دِيْن, our religion and path to God. Save us from any deed in this world that would become a source of shame and humiliation on the Day of Judgment. Lighten the burden of our sins; grant us the life of the pious; be sufficient for us in all our affairs; and protect us from the evil of the evildoers. Set us free, and set our forebears free, from the Fire of Hell, from debt, and from oppression. O You the Victorious One, Ya Aziz! O You the Forgiving One, Ya Ghaffar! O You the Generous One, Ya Kareem! O You the Coverer of Faults, Ya Sattar! O You the Forbearing One, Ya Haleem! O You the Compeller, Ya Jabbar! O You the Magnificent One, Ya Azeem! O You Who subdue all things, Ya Qahhar! O Allah! O Allah! O Allah! O You the Compassionate One in this world, Ya Rahman! O You the Merciful One in the life to come, Ya Raheem! O You the Greatest of the Merciful, Ya Arhamu ar-Rahimeen! You are the Granter of boundless mercy. And may the mercy and blessings of Allah descend always upon Muhammad (ع), the finest of all creation from eternity to eternity, and upon his descendants, and upon his companions. All praise belongs to Allah alone, the Lord of all the Universes, Rabb ul-Alameen.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 9 a prayer for the road', 'o my allah grant us steadfastness and perseverance in our دين our religion and path to god save us from any deed in this world that would become a source of shame and humiliation on the day of judgment lighten the burden of our sins grant us the life of the pious be sufficient for us in all our affairs and protect us from the evil of the evildoers set us free and set our forebears free from the fire of hell from debt and from oppression o you the victorious one ya aziz o you the forgiving one ya ghaffar o you the generous one ya kareem o you the coverer of faults ya sattar o you the forbearing one ya haleem o you the compeller ya jabbar o you the magnificent one ya azeem o you who subdue all things ya qahhar o allah o allah o allah o you the compassionate one in this world ya rahman o you the merciful one in the life to come ya raheem o you the greatest of the merciful ya arhamu ar rahimeen you are the granter of boundless mercy and may the mercy and blessings of allah descend always upon muhammad ع the finest of all creation from eternity to eternity and upon his descendants and upon his companions all praise belongs to allah alone the lord of all the universes rabb ul alameen', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'a prayer for the road', '9. A Prayer for the Road', 6, NULL, 'Here this book comes to an end, with the help of Al-Malik, the Absolute Sovereign, and Al-Wahhab, the Giver of all.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 9 a prayer for the road', 'here this book comes to an end with the help of al malik the absolute sovereign and al wahhab the giver of all', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'chapter', 'a prayer for the road', '9. A Prayer for the Road', 7, NULL, 'Editorial note (tradition-grounded). When this closing prayer petitions Allah for "the knowledge that is most beneficial," it draws on a concern the wider tradition voices directly: a related supplication preserved in this tradition asks Allah''s refuge from knowledge that brings no benefit. The same tradition sharpens why the petition matters, warning that on the Day of Judgment the most severe torment falls upon the scholar whom Allah did not benefit through his knowledge — that is, one who did not act upon what he learned. Read alongside the author''s parting charge to gather one''s resolve and act upon all the book contains, the prayer for beneficial knowledge is thus a plea not merely to know, but to be preserved from learning that stands idle and, on that Day, turns against the one who holds it.', '', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'ayyuha al walad 9 a prayer for the road', 'editorial note tradition grounded when this closing prayer petitions allah for the knowledge that is most beneficial it draws on a concern the wider tradition voices directly a related supplication preserved in this tradition asks allahs refuge from knowledge that brings no benefit the same tradition sharpens why the petition matters warning that on the day of judgment the most severe torment falls upon the scholar whom allah did not benefit through his knowledge that is one who did not act upon what he learned read alongside the authors parting charge to gather ones resolve and act upon all the book contains the prayer for beneficial knowledge is thus a plea not merely to know but to be preserved from learning that stands idle and on that day turns against the one who holds it', '');
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'episode', NULL, 'Knowledge Without Action', 0, 1, 'Knowledge Without Action The opening of Imam al-Ghazali''s letter *Ayyuha al-Walad* — the diagnosis half of his reply to a long-time disciple who wrote asking which of the many sciences he had been taught would actually save him on the Day of Judgment. Ghazali refuses the assumption that more teaching is the deficit (*if you have not received benefit from the counsels of the Prophet, then tell me — what knowledge have you truly acquired from me?*) and presses a single verdict from seven angles: the Prophet''s three warnings on useless knowledge and the forty-year clock and the scholar''s severest torment; the dream of Shaykh Junaid Baghdadi whose lifetime of discourse weighed nothing while a small number of voluntary Tahajjud prayers were of benefit; the lion-in-the- jungle parable and the unswallowed medicine; the Quranic verses on the atom''s weight and the verses of faith joined to action; the hadith that Islam is *built upon* five pillars and that faith is testimony-tongue-heart-and-action-of-limbs; the careful clarification that this is not works-righteousness but the condition of receiving Mercy (*the Mercy of Allah is close to the doers of good*); and the story of the worshipper among the Children of Israel whose unconditional service drew the forgiveness the angel''s test had pretended to withhold. The episode closes on the Prophetic hinge — *take account of yourselves before you are taken to account* — and the three closing witnesses on acting without self-admiration. The lion is coming; the swords are in the hand; whether the arm will swing is the work of the next episode.
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'episode', NULL, 'The Path of Obedience and Struggle', 0, 2, 'The Path of Obedience and Struggle The second half of the opening movement of *Ayyuha al-Walad* — the pivot from diagnosis to cure. Ghazali turns the disciple from the verdict that knowledge alone will not save him to the inhabited practice of the dawn watch: Hasan al-Basri''s involuntary sigh at the cold cup; the standing pre-dawn proclamation *is there anyone who repents, anyone who asks of Me, anyone who seeks My forgiveness?*; the Quranic command addressed directly to the Prophet ﷺ to perform *Tahajjud*; the four angels of the four stations of the night; the wind Allah has created that carries the voices of His servants at the hour before dawn; and Luqman''s testament to his own son not to let the rooster prove more intelligent than he. From the dawn watch Ghazali turns to the principle that worship is another name for obedience to the Prophetic *Shari''ah*, and works it out through four edge-cases — the fast on Eid as sin, the prayer at a forbidden hour as transgression, the prayer on unlawfully occupied ground, and the lawful intimacy between husband and wife that the uninformed would judge as appetite — to establish that the Prophetic frame is the test of whether any act is worship at all. He then turns to the inner condition: the Path cannot be travelled by accumulated knowledge, only by *mujahadah*, and the lower self must be severed with the sword of struggle. The polemic against the simulation of Sufism — and the parenthetical defense of its true practice — closes that movement. The episode ends on Ghazali''s first answer to the disciple''s second question: the four conditions of the sincere seeker, sealed by Shibli''s hadith of the four proportions which contains *the knowledge of all who came first and all who came last*. The disciple now knows what watch to keep, what rule to follow, what struggle to wage, and what four conditions frame the life.
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'episode', NULL, 'The Shaykh and the Disciple''s Rule of Life', 0, 3, 'The Shaykh and the Disciple''s Rule of Life The practical rule of life Imam al-Ghazali hands his disciple. Three braided strands. First, the eight benefits Hatim ibn Ism brought back to his master Shaykh Shafeeq al-Balkhi from long inward observation of the people of the world — each one anchored in a Quranic verse, each one the same shape: *I looked, I weighed against the Book, I changed what I did.* The only beloved who descends into the grave is one''s own righteous deeds; the Nafs must be disciplined under Quran 79:40–41; wealth must be sent ahead under Quran 16:96; Taqwa is the only true nobility under Quran 49:13; contentment with the assigned portion under Quran 43:32; Shaytan alone is the enemy under Quran 35:6 and the covenant verse; sustenance is Allah''s responsibility under Quran 11:6; Tawakkul on the One who suffices under Quran 65:3. Shaykh Shafeeq''s verdict on the eight is that whoever acts on them has acted on all four sacred Books — Tawrat, Injil, Zabur, Quran. Second, the figure at the center of the seeker''s life — the Shaykh al-Kamil, the perfected guide whose Bay''a chains unbroken back to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; the full catalog of his qualifications; the bright line that he cannot command anything forbidden by the Shari''ah; the inner etiquette of no doubt in the heart — with the honest-withdrawal clause when the inward and outward cannot agree. Third, the inner vocabulary of the Path — Ghazali''s brief, precise definitions of Tasawwuf (Shari''ah + good toward creation), servitude (obey, accept, dissolve the *I want*), Tawakkul (certitude in what is promised), and Ikhlas (deeds for Allah alone, with the metaphysical cure for Riya). The chapter closes with Ghazali''s instruction that the rest of the answers come through practice rather than further questioning — three Quranic verses on not hastening, the verb *travel* from Quran on those who refuse to set out, and Shaykh Zun Noon Misri''s line that the path is the soul actually offered, not the beautiful sayings of the Sufis. The eight admonitions belong to the next episode.
INSERT INTO search_passage (slug, kind, anchor_key, heading, ordinal, episode_number, quote, prefix, arabic, label, surah, ayah, heading_fold, body_fold, arabic_fold) VALUES ('ayyuhal-walad', 'episode', NULL, 'Eight Admonitions and a Closing Prayer', 0, 4, 'Eight Admonitions and a Closing Prayer The master''s closing brief to his beloved disciple. Eight admonitions, structured as four things to refrain from and four things to actively attend to — so that knowledge does not turn against the scholar on the Day of Judgment. The four refrainings: do not engage in religious debate (with the two narrow signs of right intention if the rare licit argument arises, and the long aside on the four categories of patients — only the obedient, sharp, intelligent seeker is worth answering); do not preach (with the *Ya Ibna Maryama* address to Jesus and the *Al-Hazr! Al-Hazr!* flood image governing what a sermon must be — fear, the Hereafter, Munkar and Nakir, the Mizan, the Sirat — and the *summoning of God''s creation from this toward that* litany governing what the preacher''s heart must seek); do not greet or even look upon kings, the wealthy, or rulers (with the hadith that praying for an oppressor''s long life is, in effect, praying for the prolongation of oppression); and do not accept their gifts even from lawful sources (with the *Shaytan*-counsel about routing money through the poor as the lure itself). The four takings-up: an intimate relationship with Allah audited by the household-servant analogy; right relating to the slaves of Allah audited by the sacred hadith *my slave''s faith is never complete until he loves for all human beings what he loves for himself*; focused study under the one-week-to-live thought experiment and the king-is-visiting house-cleaning image anchored by *Allah does not look at your faces or your outward actions, but at your hearts and your intentions*; and not accumulating more than one year''s provision, with the Prophet ﷺ''s calibrated practice for the Azwaj al-Mutahharat and ''Aisha Siddiqa. The chapter closes with the long supplication the disciple is to recite after every Salaat — the abundance block, the correction block, and the eleven divine names sealing the letter — and with the master''s final naming of Al-Malik and Al-Wahhab as the help under which the book has been written. The letter ends here.
